1
00:00:10,761 --> 00:00:13,764
<i>[indistinct chatter]</i>

2
00:00:15,641 --> 00:00:17,141
- You know this guy?
- Orlando Diaz.

3
00:00:17,142 --> 00:00:18,851
We were treating him
for DKA when he eloped.

4
00:00:18,852 --> 00:00:20,478
- How far did he fall?
- 20 feet or so.

5
00:00:20,479 --> 00:00:22,271
- Anyone see him pass out?
- No.

6
00:00:22,272 --> 00:00:24,398
They went looking for him after
he didn't answer his radio.

7
00:00:24,399 --> 00:00:25,650
- Pupils equal but sluggish.

8
00:00:25,651 --> 00:00:26,984
- He left against
medical advice.

9
00:00:26,985 --> 00:00:30,863
- On my count, three, two, one.
[grunts]

10
00:00:30,864 --> 00:00:33,157
- Dr. Ellis on the case.
Day shift, we got this.

11
00:00:33,158 --> 00:00:35,535
- I'm staying.
- Continuity of care.

12
00:00:35,536 --> 00:00:36,953
Dr. Al-Hashimi,
why don't you go cover

13
00:00:36,954 --> 00:00:38,204
sign-out rounds with Dr. Abbot?

14
00:00:38,205 --> 00:00:39,539
Shen, you too.
- Got it.

15
00:00:39,540 --> 00:00:41,624
- OK, let me know
if you need anything.

16
00:00:41,625 --> 00:00:44,210
- Lungs are up bilaterally.
- Pelvis is stable.

17
00:00:44,211 --> 00:00:48,089
Right tib-fib hematoma,
deformed right forearm.

18
00:00:48,090 --> 00:00:50,091
- Ruptured TM on the left
with sero-sanguinous fluid.

19
00:00:50,092 --> 00:00:51,884
- That means
basilar skull fracture.

20
00:00:51,885 --> 00:00:53,678
- We've got a diabetic patient,
20-foot fall.

21
00:00:53,679 --> 00:00:56,013
- BP 204 over 98, pulse 56.

22
00:00:56,014 --> 00:00:57,682
- Dr. Mohan, what do those
numbers indicate?

23
00:00:57,683 --> 00:00:59,517
- I'm trying
to treat the patient.

24
00:00:59,518 --> 00:01:00,810
- It's the Cushing's reflex.

25
00:01:00,811 --> 00:01:02,895
- From increased
intracranial pressure.

26
00:01:02,896 --> 00:01:05,231
- Yeah, we need to get him
to CT right away.

27
00:01:05,232 --> 00:01:06,899
- No free fluid in the belly.

28
00:01:06,900 --> 00:01:10,236
- Any family to notify?
- Wife and three kids.

29
00:01:10,237 --> 00:01:12,363
- Is there a card
to sign for Robby?

30
00:01:12,364 --> 00:01:14,365
- No, he said
no card, no presents.

31
00:01:14,366 --> 00:01:15,783
- Oh. Was there a cake?

32
00:01:15,784 --> 00:01:17,368
- Oh, he didn't want
that either.

33
00:01:17,369 --> 00:01:19,328
- That sucks.
I need some cake.

34
00:01:19,329 --> 00:01:21,539
- Patient was injured
when they raided a restaurant.

35
00:01:21,540 --> 00:01:23,332
- And they actually
arrested the nurse?

36
00:01:23,333 --> 00:01:25,876
- With a takedown and zip ties.
- Damn.

37
00:01:25,877 --> 00:01:28,045
- Crus, you, uh, know
the computer downtime drill?

38
00:01:28,046 --> 00:01:29,463
- Yeah, no biggie.

39
00:01:29,464 --> 00:01:31,173
I prefer to treat 'em
without labs and X-rays.

40
00:01:31,174 --> 00:01:32,675
Welcome back.

41
00:01:32,676 --> 00:01:34,552
- If you do have orders
for labs and X-rays,

42
00:01:34,553 --> 00:01:36,596
clipboards go here.

43
00:01:36,597 --> 00:01:39,390
If it's just
nursing orders, here.

44
00:01:39,391 --> 00:01:41,183
- Please write neatly,
for God's sake.

45
00:01:41,184 --> 00:01:43,728
- Oh, Monica, this is Nazely,
new intern.

46
00:01:43,729 --> 00:01:46,565
- I don't need to know that.
- So nice to meet you.

47
00:01:47,649 --> 00:01:49,400
- Looks like you and me, Jack.

48
00:01:49,401 --> 00:01:50,818
- Yeah, who's our senior?

49
00:01:50,819 --> 00:01:53,946
- Dr. Henderson.
- Excellent.

50
00:01:53,947 --> 00:01:55,823
Night shift's on Crus control.

51
00:01:55,824 --> 00:01:57,950
- You hear
ICE brought us a patient?

52
00:01:57,951 --> 00:01:59,744
- Oh, yeah?
How'd that go?

53
00:01:59,745 --> 00:02:00,911
- There was some
collateral damage.

54
00:02:00,912 --> 00:02:02,413
They arrested Jesse.

55
00:02:02,414 --> 00:02:04,415
- For what?
- Assaulting a federal officer.

56
00:02:04,416 --> 00:02:05,916
- Are you fucking kidding me?

57
00:02:05,917 --> 00:02:07,293
- Oh, Dr. Al-Hashimi,

58
00:02:07,294 --> 00:02:09,587
this is Nazely Toomarian--
new intern.

59
00:02:09,588 --> 00:02:12,633
[both speaking Armenian]

60
00:02:16,303 --> 00:02:18,262
- OK, everybody,
let's huddle up.

61
00:02:18,263 --> 00:02:19,597
- Before we start,

62
00:02:19,598 --> 00:02:21,182
let me address
the elephant in the room.

63
00:02:21,183 --> 00:02:22,975
For those of you
who don't know,

64
00:02:22,976 --> 00:02:24,435
Jesse tried to intervene

65
00:02:24,436 --> 00:02:26,270
when a federal agent
was handling his patient,

66
00:02:26,271 --> 00:02:28,606
and both he and the patient
were taken away by ICE.

67
00:02:28,607 --> 00:02:30,274
- Did he punch the guy?
- No.

68
00:02:30,275 --> 00:02:32,735
He put out a hand to stop him,
made physical contact.

69
00:02:32,736 --> 00:02:34,278
- [softly] It was a little bit
more than that.

70
00:02:34,279 --> 00:02:35,905
- The hospital lawyers
are on the case,

71
00:02:35,906 --> 00:02:38,240
and we will brief the staff
every 12 hours

72
00:02:38,241 --> 00:02:39,950
at sign-out rounds
with an official update.

73
00:02:39,951 --> 00:02:42,453
- In the meantime,
don't feed the rumor mill.

74
00:02:42,454 --> 00:02:44,789
- You may have also heard
that new grad nurse Emma here

75
00:02:44,790 --> 00:02:47,291
was assaulted by a patient.
- I--I'm fine.

76
00:02:47,292 --> 00:02:48,959
- Please don't forget
"code hula hoop,"

77
00:02:48,960 --> 00:02:50,294
and with risky patients,

78
00:02:50,295 --> 00:02:52,630
always keep a path
to the door clear.

79
00:02:52,631 --> 00:02:54,298
- Sounds like
one hell of a day.

80
00:02:54,299 --> 00:02:56,133
And we're still dealing
with downtime,

81
00:02:56,134 --> 00:02:58,302
so day shift should brief
night residents

82
00:02:58,303 --> 00:02:59,345
about the protocol--
[clicking]

83
00:02:59,346 --> 00:03:00,554
[crowd murmuring]
- Hey!

84
00:03:00,555 --> 00:03:01,889
- Oh, all right!

85
00:03:01,890 --> 00:03:04,475
[applause]
- Oh, thank God.

86
00:03:04,476 --> 00:03:05,810
- Yeah, not so fast.

87
00:03:05,811 --> 00:03:07,687
Every chart
from the last five hours

88
00:03:07,688 --> 00:03:10,022
needs to be scanned into
the Electronic Health Record

89
00:03:10,023 --> 00:03:12,108
and checked for accuracy...

90
00:03:12,109 --> 00:03:14,276
- Um, whose job is that?
- Day shift.

91
00:03:14,277 --> 00:03:16,195
- With a completed
and signed T-sheet.

92
00:03:16,196 --> 00:03:17,947
- Are you kidding me?
- That's gonna take forever.

93
00:03:17,948 --> 00:03:19,657
- We will set up stations
for scanning.

94
00:03:19,658 --> 00:03:21,492
OK, everybody,
let's get moving.

95
00:03:21,493 --> 00:03:23,661
- Triage is 20 behind.
Gotta help 'em clean up.

96
00:03:23,662 --> 00:03:24,870
I'm skipping rounds.

97
00:03:24,871 --> 00:03:26,706
- Consider yourself lucky.

98
00:03:26,707 --> 00:03:29,333
- Should we join them?
- [sighs]

99
00:03:29,334 --> 00:03:31,001
Might as well
witness the magic.

100
00:03:31,002 --> 00:03:33,254
It's like changing of the guard
at Buckingham Palace.

101
00:03:33,255 --> 00:03:35,172
- Except we're allowed
to smile.

102
00:03:35,173 --> 00:03:37,383
- "Smile though
your heart is aching."

103
00:03:37,384 --> 00:03:38,509
[machine beeping]

104
00:03:38,510 --> 00:03:39,635
- Plain films, right tib-fib

105
00:03:39,636 --> 00:03:41,554
and right forearm after CT.

106
00:03:41,555 --> 00:03:43,556
- Got the chem-8.
Blood sugar's 284.

107
00:03:43,557 --> 00:03:46,183
- That's not too bad.
Potassium looks good.

108
00:03:46,184 --> 00:03:48,477
- Mm-hmm.
Anion gap's up at 14.

109
00:03:48,478 --> 00:03:49,687
- It was 24 this morning.

110
00:03:49,688 --> 00:03:51,355
He's definitely improving.

111
00:03:51,356 --> 00:03:52,732
- The DKA is resolving.

112
00:03:52,733 --> 00:03:54,358
- So what made him pass out?

113
00:03:54,359 --> 00:03:56,193
- It's hot as hell out there.

114
00:03:56,194 --> 00:03:58,362
He could have been dehydrated
from sweating.

115
00:03:58,363 --> 00:04:01,866
Or he could have had
an N-STEMI or a posterior CVA.

116
00:04:01,867 --> 00:04:03,326
- Ready to roll.

117
00:04:05,036 --> 00:04:07,747
- I can babysit him at CT.
- Thank you.

118
00:04:07,748 --> 00:04:10,041
- I'll stay
in case he needs the OR.

119
00:04:11,835 --> 00:04:13,753
- Hey, we're back up.

120
00:04:13,754 --> 00:04:15,755
- It's about time.

121
00:04:15,756 --> 00:04:17,214
- Ooh, Robby.
- Yeah.

122
00:04:17,215 --> 00:04:19,425
- I got intel on where
ICE has taken Jesse.

123
00:04:19,426 --> 00:04:20,718
- Intel from who?

124
00:04:20,719 --> 00:04:22,553
- Uh, my brother-in-law's
a cop.

125
00:04:22,554 --> 00:04:24,889
So he'll be processed
at DHS Southside

126
00:04:24,890 --> 00:04:27,391
and then transferred
to ICE detention in Clearfield.

127
00:04:27,392 --> 00:04:28,726
- Clearfield?
That's two hours away.

128
00:04:28,727 --> 00:04:30,227
- Mm-hmm.

129
00:04:30,228 --> 00:04:32,313
- OK, I'll let
the hospital attorneys know.

130
00:04:32,314 --> 00:04:34,231
- How are you doing, Oliver?
- Alive and kicking.

131
00:04:34,232 --> 00:04:35,608
- Uh, Mr. Haas.

132
00:04:35,609 --> 00:04:37,902
Pulmonary edema
after missed dialysis,

133
00:04:37,903 --> 00:04:40,529
now stable with his son Mason
at the bedside.

134
00:04:40,530 --> 00:04:42,114
- Hello.
- I'm Dr. Shen.

135
00:04:42,115 --> 00:04:43,365
I'll be back.

136
00:04:43,366 --> 00:04:44,408
- Dialysis tech says

137
00:04:44,409 --> 00:04:46,202
I'm off the machine at 10:42.

138
00:04:46,203 --> 00:04:48,287
- Hit the call light,
and I'll come running.

139
00:04:48,288 --> 00:04:50,415
- South 21 next...

140
00:04:52,375 --> 00:04:54,168
Mr. Digby.

141
00:04:54,169 --> 00:04:55,753
- J-just Digby.

142
00:04:55,754 --> 00:04:57,421
- Dr. Mohan's patient.

143
00:04:57,422 --> 00:04:59,632
Forearm cellulitis,
admit med-surge.

144
00:04:59,633 --> 00:05:01,258
Awaiting a bed.
- I can take him.

145
00:05:01,259 --> 00:05:03,093
- Sold to Dr. Toomarian.

146
00:05:03,094 --> 00:05:05,471
Moving right along.

147
00:05:05,472 --> 00:05:07,264
- Get enough to eat?

148
00:05:07,265 --> 00:05:09,600
We'll be back.

149
00:05:09,601 --> 00:05:13,062
- Lillian Stegman.
Tweaked her knee water-skiing.

150
00:05:13,063 --> 00:05:15,105
- Doing a 360
off a 5-foot ramp.

151
00:05:15,106 --> 00:05:16,565
- Ooh, respect.

152
00:05:16,566 --> 00:05:17,900
- X-rays ordered in triage.

153
00:05:17,901 --> 00:05:19,151
- That was hours ago.

154
00:05:19,152 --> 00:05:21,403
- Ma'am,
I'm Dr. Crus Henderson.

155
00:05:21,404 --> 00:05:23,614
Let's get you home real fast.

156
00:05:23,615 --> 00:05:25,366
- That would be wonderful.
- OK, heading to North.

157
00:05:25,367 --> 00:05:27,117
- Thank you.

158
00:05:27,118 --> 00:05:29,620
- Looks like our first scanning
station is almost ready.

159
00:05:29,621 --> 00:05:32,373
Whitaker, Santos, after rounds,
this will be your post.

160
00:05:32,374 --> 00:05:35,042
- Seriously?
- For... how long?

161
00:05:35,043 --> 00:05:37,169
- Till we mop up this mess.

162
00:05:37,170 --> 00:05:38,838
And away we go.

163
00:05:38,839 --> 00:05:41,006
- All right,
now you know everything

164
00:05:41,007 --> 00:05:42,258
there is to know about rounds.

165
00:05:42,259 --> 00:05:43,425
Fascinating, right?

166
00:05:43,426 --> 00:05:45,594
- Uh... yeah.

167
00:05:45,595 --> 00:05:47,806
- Here, I got something
better for you.

168
00:05:49,599 --> 00:05:52,476
So, Digby, we're gonna find you
some new clothes,

169
00:05:52,477 --> 00:05:54,270
but my friend Emma and I
wondered if you'd like

170
00:05:54,271 --> 00:05:56,856
to get cleaned up a bit first.

171
00:05:56,857 --> 00:05:58,649
- I already had a shower.

172
00:05:58,650 --> 00:06:00,234
- I know, and you look good.

173
00:06:00,235 --> 00:06:02,319
But the offer also comes
with a shave and a haircut,

174
00:06:02,320 --> 00:06:03,571
if you're interested.

175
00:06:03,572 --> 00:06:04,822
No pressure--like I said,

176
00:06:04,823 --> 00:06:05,991
you already look great.

177
00:06:07,367 --> 00:06:10,369
- Sure, I guess.
- Fantastic.

178
00:06:10,370 --> 00:06:12,371
All right, kid, fill that basin
with some warm water

179
00:06:12,372 --> 00:06:14,040
and lather him up.

180
00:06:16,960 --> 00:06:19,461
- Hey, stay safe out there.
- Back at you in here.

181
00:06:19,462 --> 00:06:21,463
- Hey, Cary.

182
00:06:21,464 --> 00:06:23,549
You were on scene
with Orlando, right?

183
00:06:23,550 --> 00:06:26,010
Did you notice anything
about his fall?

184
00:06:26,011 --> 00:06:27,511
- Like what?

185
00:06:27,512 --> 00:06:30,681
- Like, um, if there was
a break in the railing

186
00:06:30,682 --> 00:06:33,684
or if there were
security cameras?

187
00:06:33,685 --> 00:06:35,227
- Too busy working on the guy--

188
00:06:35,228 --> 00:06:37,688
IV, intubation,
backboard, splint.

189
00:06:37,689 --> 00:06:38,856
- Thanks.

190
00:06:38,857 --> 00:06:40,524
- Oh, two names is a start.

191
00:06:40,525 --> 00:06:42,234
Pretty soon, we'll retire
the clipboards.

192
00:06:42,235 --> 00:06:43,569
- Baby Jane Doe in Pedes?

193
00:06:43,570 --> 00:06:45,487
- Uh, yeah, abandoned

194
00:06:45,488 --> 00:06:48,198
in the waiting room bathroom
at 7:30 a.m.

195
00:06:48,199 --> 00:06:50,701
- Safe haven drop-off?
- No, she's too old for that.

196
00:06:50,702 --> 00:06:52,536
At least two months.
- I'll take her.

197
00:06:52,537 --> 00:06:54,705
- Awaiting foster placement
by CYF.

198
00:06:54,706 --> 00:06:57,041
- What if the mom comes back?
- Call the cops.

199
00:06:57,042 --> 00:06:58,459
- Day shift,
get to work scanning.

200
00:06:58,460 --> 00:07:00,044
Night shift, the room numbers

201
00:07:00,045 --> 00:07:02,212
on top of the dry-erase board
need a doctor.

202
00:07:02,213 --> 00:07:05,090
- Who's in BH-1?
- Psych hold, danger to self.

203
00:07:05,091 --> 00:07:06,550
- What's the story?

204
00:07:06,551 --> 00:07:08,427
- Doctors King and Langdon
were on the case.

205
00:07:08,428 --> 00:07:10,679
- It's a sad story--
she says her 5-year-old

206
00:07:10,680 --> 00:07:12,556
snuck into the back seat
of the car to take a nap.

207
00:07:12,557 --> 00:07:14,224
- Heat stroke.

208
00:07:14,225 --> 00:07:16,602
- Mom was extremely distraught.
Walked out into traffic.

209
00:07:16,603 --> 00:07:18,187
- Grady Barnhill, wheezing

210
00:07:18,188 --> 00:07:19,772
and not responding
to albuterol.

211
00:07:19,773 --> 00:07:21,690
Pulse ox 87. His mother, Naomi.

212
00:07:21,691 --> 00:07:23,859
- OK, Trauma Two's open.
- On our way.

213
00:07:23,860 --> 00:07:26,403
Uh, Mom, history of asthma?
- His whole life.

214
00:07:26,404 --> 00:07:28,489
Never this bad.
- Barely moving air.

215
00:07:28,490 --> 00:07:31,116
- Using all his
accessory muscles.

216
00:07:31,117 --> 00:07:32,576
- Call RT!

217
00:07:32,577 --> 00:07:35,329
Uh, continuous nebs,
20 milligrams an hour.

218
00:07:35,330 --> 00:07:36,830
- We have a home nebulizer.

219
00:07:36,831 --> 00:07:39,750
I've been giving him
albuterol every hour.

220
00:07:39,751 --> 00:07:40,834
- Ready?

221
00:07:40,835 --> 00:07:43,754
On my count, one, two, three.

222
00:07:43,755 --> 00:07:45,255
- Does he take
any other medications?

223
00:07:45,256 --> 00:07:46,757
- Just the albuterol.

224
00:07:46,758 --> 00:07:48,258
He used to be on montelukast
and Symbicort.

225
00:07:48,259 --> 00:07:49,885
- Why did you stop that?

226
00:07:49,886 --> 00:07:51,929
- Because he lost his Medicaid
two months ago.

227
00:07:51,930 --> 00:07:53,764
- He's so tight,
nothing's getting through.

228
00:07:53,765 --> 00:07:55,307
- Mel, EpiPen.
- On it.

229
00:07:55,308 --> 00:07:56,809
- 0.3 milligrams.

230
00:07:56,810 --> 00:07:59,937
- That may open his lungs
so the nebs can work.

231
00:07:59,938 --> 00:08:01,981
- All right, Grady,
shot in the thigh.

232
00:08:01,982 --> 00:08:04,191
- [grunts]

233
00:08:04,192 --> 00:08:06,986
[beeping]

234
00:08:06,987 --> 00:08:08,821
- Hey.

235
00:08:08,822 --> 00:08:10,280
- Something to scan?

236
00:08:10,281 --> 00:08:11,824
- Critical labs on North Three.

237
00:08:11,825 --> 00:08:13,450
- Oh, it's, uh--
it's not my patient.

238
00:08:13,451 --> 00:08:14,786
- She's about to be.

239
00:08:16,621 --> 00:08:18,956
- I'm, uh--
I'm off the clock, so...

240
00:08:18,957 --> 00:08:21,583
- These were ordered
three hours ago from triage,

241
00:08:21,584 --> 00:08:24,086
so, technically,
she is a day-shift patient.

242
00:08:24,087 --> 00:08:25,337
Just get the intern started.

243
00:08:25,338 --> 00:08:26,922
I got a meeting upstairs
with admin

244
00:08:26,923 --> 00:08:28,924
to get things back on track.

245
00:08:28,925 --> 00:08:30,634
- Sorry.
Everyone else is tied up.

246
00:08:30,635 --> 00:08:32,011
- That's OK.

247
00:08:32,012 --> 00:08:33,220
How'd you get stuck
on night shift

248
00:08:33,221 --> 00:08:34,388
for your first rotation?

249
00:08:34,389 --> 00:08:35,848
- Just lucky, I guess.

250
00:08:35,849 --> 00:08:37,391
- Yeah, if you like
a good horror movie.

251
00:08:37,392 --> 00:08:38,726
- [chuckles]

252
00:08:38,727 --> 00:08:40,686
- Where was med school?
- Uh, UC Irvine.

253
00:08:40,687 --> 00:08:42,479
- California, right?
- Yep.

254
00:08:42,480 --> 00:08:43,731
- Pittsburgh's
a little different.

255
00:08:43,732 --> 00:08:44,982
- Yeah, a lot more humidity.

256
00:08:44,983 --> 00:08:46,025
- Yeah, that'll go away

257
00:08:46,026 --> 00:08:47,985
with your first foot of snow.

258
00:08:47,986 --> 00:08:50,738
Uh, hi, Mrs. Davis.

259
00:08:50,739 --> 00:08:52,823
I am Dr. McKay.
This is Doctor...

260
00:08:52,824 --> 00:08:55,034
- Toomarian.
- Hello.

261
00:08:55,035 --> 00:08:58,162
- Uh, your labs show a lot
of inflammation in your liver.

262
00:08:58,163 --> 00:09:01,540
- Is that why I'm so nauseated?
- Definitely, yeah.

263
00:09:01,541 --> 00:09:04,668
Have you, um, noticed a change
in your skin coloration?

264
00:09:04,669 --> 00:09:06,670
- I've been using
this bronzing cream

265
00:09:06,671 --> 00:09:09,339
so I don't look like a ghost
in my bathing suit.

266
00:09:09,340 --> 00:09:11,341
- Do you remember
the name of the bronzer?

267
00:09:11,342 --> 00:09:12,843
It could have a chemical
that can cause--

268
00:09:12,844 --> 00:09:14,887
- Oh, no, no,
it's 100% natural.

269
00:09:14,888 --> 00:09:18,182
It's DHA from sugar beets.
- OK.

270
00:09:18,183 --> 00:09:20,350
- Why would my liver
be inflamed?

271
00:09:20,351 --> 00:09:22,311
- Uh, there's a lot
of possibilities.

272
00:09:22,312 --> 00:09:24,146
- Are you taking any
prescription medications?

273
00:09:24,147 --> 00:09:25,147
- Not a single one.

274
00:09:25,148 --> 00:09:26,690
- Tylenol?
- God, no.

275
00:09:26,691 --> 00:09:28,901
- We have to ask everybody
about injection drug use.

276
00:09:28,902 --> 00:09:30,027
- Never.

277
00:09:30,028 --> 00:09:31,695
- Raw clams or oysters?

278
00:09:31,696 --> 00:09:33,530
- I'm a vegan.

279
00:09:33,531 --> 00:09:35,741
- One step at a time, OK?

280
00:09:35,742 --> 00:09:36,910
We'll be back.

281
00:09:38,244 --> 00:09:40,204
- Digby, Haas,
now on the big board.

282
00:09:40,205 --> 00:09:41,163
- Progress.

283
00:09:41,164 --> 00:09:42,164
- So he's just been waiting

284
00:09:42,165 --> 00:09:43,874
in the hall the whole time?

285
00:09:43,875 --> 00:09:46,753
OK, well, can you maybe
get to him next?

286
00:09:48,004 --> 00:09:50,214
Thank you. [sighs]

287
00:09:50,215 --> 00:09:51,840
Duke goes to CT,
and they bump him.

288
00:09:51,841 --> 00:09:54,343
- Yeah, he may end up
waiting for hours over there.

289
00:09:54,344 --> 00:09:56,887
- Oh, look at that.
7:15 already.

290
00:09:56,888 --> 00:09:58,806
- Just a few more loose ends
to tie up.

291
00:09:58,807 --> 00:10:02,059
- You got more loose ends
than a macramé wall hanging.

292
00:10:02,060 --> 00:10:04,353
My mom had one.
Took up the whole goddamn wall.

293
00:10:04,354 --> 00:10:06,063
Seriously, you should
think about signing out

294
00:10:06,064 --> 00:10:07,856
and hitting the road.
- Yeah, maybe you should too.

295
00:10:07,857 --> 00:10:09,566
Oh, that's right,
you sent Lena home.

296
00:10:09,567 --> 00:10:11,443
Are you planning on
calling in a replacement,

297
00:10:11,444 --> 00:10:13,237
or are you just gonna work
till sunrise?

298
00:10:13,238 --> 00:10:14,696
- If I have to.

299
00:10:14,697 --> 00:10:16,740
- Oh, so you get to go
the extra mile,

300
00:10:16,741 --> 00:10:19,243
and the rest of us just get
accused of being martyrs.

301
00:10:19,244 --> 00:10:20,620
- Robby.
- Excuse me.

302
00:10:22,580 --> 00:10:24,581
What's the word?
- No subdural, no epidural.

303
00:10:24,582 --> 00:10:26,583
- Diffuse brain swelling
with effacement

304
00:10:26,584 --> 00:10:28,252
and compression
of the ventricles.

305
00:10:28,253 --> 00:10:30,462
- C-spine?
Chest-abdomen-pelvis?

306
00:10:30,463 --> 00:10:32,756
- Uh, left pulmonary contusion,
no intra-abdominal hemorrhage.

307
00:10:32,757 --> 00:10:33,924
- Which means I'm off the hook.

308
00:10:33,925 --> 00:10:35,425
I'll send Neurosurgery down.

309
00:10:35,426 --> 00:10:37,928
- If he doesn't need the OR,
why Neurosurgery?

310
00:10:37,929 --> 00:10:39,346
- Wait and see.
- Let's get busy.

311
00:10:39,347 --> 00:10:40,597
CVP and an art line.

312
00:10:40,598 --> 00:10:43,767
- Orlando.
Orlando, squeeze my hand.

313
00:10:43,768 --> 00:10:46,145
[beeping]
Open your eyes.

314
00:10:46,146 --> 00:10:48,689
- We already know his GCS
is going to be three.

315
00:10:48,690 --> 00:10:50,691
- Hey, Javadi,
anti-seizure prophylaxis?

316
00:10:50,692 --> 00:10:52,693
- Keppra, 20 migs per kilo.
- Mixing it now.

317
00:10:52,694 --> 00:10:54,361
- Gotta get this brain swelling
down, Samira.

318
00:10:54,362 --> 00:10:55,488
How we gonna do that?

319
00:10:56,781 --> 00:10:58,323
Samira?
- What?

320
00:10:58,324 --> 00:11:01,535
- We have to lower
the intracranial pressure.

321
00:11:01,536 --> 00:11:03,287
- Mannitol.
- Not the best.

322
00:11:03,288 --> 00:11:04,621
Can cause diuresis
and hypotension.

323
00:11:04,622 --> 00:11:05,956
- Then hypertonic saline.

324
00:11:05,957 --> 00:11:08,458
- 23%.
50 cc's through a central line.

325
00:11:08,459 --> 00:11:10,294
[beeping]

326
00:11:10,295 --> 00:11:11,920
- Still tight as a drum.

327
00:11:11,921 --> 00:11:13,005
- Five minutes since the epi.

328
00:11:13,006 --> 00:11:14,423
- He can have one more EpiPen.

329
00:11:14,424 --> 00:11:16,133
- Sats are borderline, 87.

330
00:11:16,134 --> 00:11:17,593
- How about BiPap?
10 over 5.

331
00:11:17,594 --> 00:11:19,761
- Yes, with in-line nebs.

332
00:11:19,762 --> 00:11:21,597
- All right, Grady,
shot number two.

333
00:11:21,598 --> 00:11:23,807
- Might as well throw in
some magnesium, 50 per kilo.

334
00:11:23,808 --> 00:11:25,142
- I'm sorry.
He's not ventilating.

335
00:11:25,143 --> 00:11:26,476
We should be ready to intubate.

336
00:11:26,477 --> 00:11:27,853
- With an asthma patient?

337
00:11:27,854 --> 00:11:29,771
There's a high risk
of cardiac arrest.

338
00:11:29,772 --> 00:11:32,024
- Naomi, has he ever been put
on a breathing machine before?

339
00:11:32,025 --> 00:11:33,692
- Never.
- Hospitalized?

340
00:11:33,693 --> 00:11:35,652
- No, we could always break him
with the Symbicort.

341
00:11:35,653 --> 00:11:37,112
- We're gonna give
the current treatment

342
00:11:37,113 --> 00:11:39,156
a few more minutes to help him
turn the corner.

343
00:11:39,157 --> 00:11:42,326
[machines beeping]

344
00:11:42,327 --> 00:11:44,203
- I could probably
do this myself

345
00:11:44,204 --> 00:11:45,662
now that my cast is off.

346
00:11:45,663 --> 00:11:47,539
- Oh, that's OK.

347
00:11:47,540 --> 00:11:49,209
I don't mind.

348
00:11:53,087 --> 00:11:55,006
- How long have you
been a nurse?

349
00:11:56,382 --> 00:11:58,092
- This is my first day.

350
00:12:00,762 --> 00:12:02,805
- You're doing really good.

351
00:12:04,724 --> 00:12:06,309
- Thank you.

352
00:12:10,313 --> 00:12:12,064
- Oh, shit, you OK?
- Yeah.

353
00:12:12,065 --> 00:12:13,565
There's water on the floor.

354
00:12:13,566 --> 00:12:15,067
- Sorry!
I--I might have spilled.

355
00:12:15,068 --> 00:12:16,526
- Yeah, no worries.

356
00:12:16,527 --> 00:12:18,528
We're gonna have to sort
these all by patient

357
00:12:18,529 --> 00:12:20,322
before we scan them.

358
00:12:20,323 --> 00:12:23,033
- Is, uh, Joy still around?

359
00:12:23,034 --> 00:12:25,702
- I heard she left at 6:59.

360
00:12:25,703 --> 00:12:27,704
- Smart girl.
- Mm-hmm.

361
00:12:27,705 --> 00:12:29,873
- You know what? Find Ogilvie.
He's such an ass-kisser.

362
00:12:29,874 --> 00:12:31,500
He might stay and scan
all the charts himself.

363
00:12:31,501 --> 00:12:33,710
- Oh, he, uh, scrubbed in
in the OR,

364
00:12:33,711 --> 00:12:35,587
scrubbed in
on the ruptured triple-A.

365
00:12:35,588 --> 00:12:37,339
- Oh, the kidney stone
that wasn't.

366
00:12:37,340 --> 00:12:39,133
That'll be four hours at least.

367
00:12:41,052 --> 00:12:44,471
So are you still going
to Amy's after work?

368
00:12:44,472 --> 00:12:46,390
- Yeah, maybe just
for tonight,

369
00:12:46,391 --> 00:12:48,768
after Robby briefs me
on all the house stuff.

370
00:12:49,811 --> 00:12:51,311
- Sounds fun.

371
00:12:51,312 --> 00:12:52,646
- [scoffs]

372
00:12:52,647 --> 00:12:54,648
When he asked,
it was hard to say no.

373
00:12:54,649 --> 00:12:56,234
- Sure. [sighs]

374
00:12:58,236 --> 00:13:00,487
It's gonna be very weird
without him here.

375
00:13:00,488 --> 00:13:01,905
- Yeah.

376
00:13:01,906 --> 00:13:04,449
You think Dr. Al-Hashimi
can handle it?

377
00:13:04,450 --> 00:13:05,993
- In a word, no.

378
00:13:05,994 --> 00:13:07,411
- She's smart.

379
00:13:07,412 --> 00:13:08,745
- It takes more than that.

380
00:13:08,746 --> 00:13:10,330
- Yeah, well, at least

381
00:13:10,331 --> 00:13:11,748
it's only three months.

382
00:13:11,749 --> 00:13:13,875
- Yeah, three long months.

383
00:13:13,876 --> 00:13:15,585
Then you need to find
a new home.

384
00:13:15,586 --> 00:13:17,879
- [chuckles]
Not necessarily.

385
00:13:17,880 --> 00:13:19,965
I mean, if I wanted,
I could just check on

386
00:13:19,966 --> 00:13:23,093
Robby's house once
or twice a day, you know.

387
00:13:23,094 --> 00:13:24,804
- Why would you do that?

388
00:13:26,097 --> 00:13:28,307
- I don't know. Why not?

389
00:13:28,308 --> 00:13:31,310
[beeping, whirring]

390
00:13:31,311 --> 00:13:32,769
- Hypertonic's in.

391
00:13:32,770 --> 00:13:34,938
- Maintenance LR in the AC.

392
00:13:34,939 --> 00:13:36,732
- Looks like a halo sign.

393
00:13:36,733 --> 00:13:39,276
- Cerebrospinal fluid
from a basilar skull fracture.

394
00:13:39,277 --> 00:13:43,280
- The halo sign is neither
sensitive nor specific for CSF.

395
00:13:43,281 --> 00:13:45,615
Saline, tap water,
and runny noses

396
00:13:45,616 --> 00:13:46,783
all separate from blood.

397
00:13:46,784 --> 00:13:48,285
- Hello, Linda.
- Robby.

398
00:13:48,286 --> 00:13:50,787
- Division chief working
on a holiday weekend.

399
00:13:50,788 --> 00:13:52,873
- Well, somebody's got to.

400
00:13:52,874 --> 00:13:55,667
Saw the scans.
This guy needs an EVD.

401
00:13:55,668 --> 00:13:57,961
- External ventricular drain
to take down the pressure.

402
00:13:57,962 --> 00:14:01,214
- Because CPP equals
"MAP" minus ICP.

403
00:14:01,215 --> 00:14:03,884
- Cerebral perfusion pressure
in the brain

404
00:14:03,885 --> 00:14:05,802
equals mean arterial
blood pressure

405
00:14:05,803 --> 00:14:07,304
minus the pressure
in the skull.

406
00:14:07,305 --> 00:14:09,014
- ICP less than 22

407
00:14:09,015 --> 00:14:11,558
predicts survival
and favorable outcome.

408
00:14:11,559 --> 00:14:15,979
CPP from 60 to 70 reduces
morbidity and mortality.

409
00:14:15,980 --> 00:14:17,356
Can somebody assist?

410
00:14:17,357 --> 00:14:18,523
- Dr. Mohan?

411
00:14:18,524 --> 00:14:19,692
- I'm good.

412
00:14:20,777 --> 00:14:22,319
- Samira, you should
lend a hand.

413
00:14:22,320 --> 00:14:24,112
- I said I'm good.

414
00:14:24,113 --> 00:14:26,990
- Javadi?
- I don't think so.

415
00:14:26,991 --> 00:14:28,492
- Javadi?

416
00:14:28,493 --> 00:14:31,953
As in, daughter
of Raymond and Eileen?

417
00:14:31,954 --> 00:14:33,955
I was at your mother's
baby shower.

418
00:14:33,956 --> 00:14:35,332
[chuckles] She was a resident.

419
00:14:35,333 --> 00:14:36,751
You were in utero.

420
00:14:38,211 --> 00:14:39,336
I don't know how
she got through

421
00:14:39,337 --> 00:14:41,171
motherhood and training.

422
00:14:41,172 --> 00:14:42,714
Feel free to join me.

423
00:14:42,715 --> 00:14:44,633
- Oh, my--my shift was over
a while ago.

424
00:14:44,634 --> 00:14:46,051
- OK.

425
00:14:46,052 --> 00:14:48,095
Masks all around.
Two sterile gowns.

426
00:14:48,096 --> 00:14:50,722
We brought the surgical towels.
Just need some Betadine.

427
00:14:50,723 --> 00:14:52,392
- This is kind of
an amazing opportunity.

428
00:14:53,518 --> 00:14:54,768
- I'm exhausted.

429
00:14:54,769 --> 00:14:56,728
- The Chief of Neurosurgery
is here.

430
00:14:56,729 --> 00:14:58,188
She's kind of a legend.

431
00:14:58,189 --> 00:15:00,232
- Maybe one of the night
residents can do it.

432
00:15:00,233 --> 00:15:02,526
- Is this about
the volvulus case from earlier?

433
00:15:02,527 --> 00:15:04,069
'Cause if it is,
you made a mistake.

434
00:15:04,070 --> 00:15:06,029
You need to move on.

435
00:15:06,030 --> 00:15:07,697
I do not want to come back here
and find that

436
00:15:07,698 --> 00:15:10,867
you've applied
for a residency in derm.

437
00:15:10,868 --> 00:15:13,537
Step up to the plate. Do it.

438
00:15:13,538 --> 00:15:14,746
Javadi will assist.

439
00:15:14,747 --> 00:15:16,415
- Excellent.

440
00:15:16,416 --> 00:15:18,208
You know, I think Eileen did
her first EVD with me

441
00:15:18,209 --> 00:15:20,710
back when I was a brand-new
assistant professor.

442
00:15:20,711 --> 00:15:22,045
[scoffs]

443
00:15:22,046 --> 00:15:23,548
Time flies.

444
00:15:25,216 --> 00:15:26,425
- [raspy breathing]

445
00:15:26,426 --> 00:15:27,551
- How you doing, Grady?

446
00:15:27,552 --> 00:15:29,052
- Still retracting.

447
00:15:29,053 --> 00:15:30,720
- Any improvement?
- Not really.

448
00:15:30,721 --> 00:15:31,889
- You feeling tired?

449
00:15:33,683 --> 00:15:36,226
- Do we have Aerogen in the ED?
- Just in the unit.

450
00:15:36,227 --> 00:15:37,894
- How fast can you get back?

451
00:15:37,895 --> 00:15:39,062
- Two minutes
if I take the stairs.

452
00:15:39,063 --> 00:15:40,063
- Go.

453
00:15:40,064 --> 00:15:41,398
- Uh, Aerogen?

454
00:15:41,399 --> 00:15:43,358
- It has a 5-millimeter mesh

455
00:15:43,359 --> 00:15:46,612
that vibrates
over 120,000 times per second.

456
00:15:51,742 --> 00:15:53,119
- Dr. Al-Hashimi?

457
00:15:57,415 --> 00:15:58,833
- You OK?

458
00:16:01,252 --> 00:16:03,296
- Uh, you were telling us
about Aerogen.

459
00:16:04,422 --> 00:16:05,840
- Dr. Shen can explain.

460
00:16:07,175 --> 00:16:10,135
- It produces droplets
in the 2- to 5-micron range

461
00:16:10,136 --> 00:16:12,096
for medication delivery.

462
00:16:19,395 --> 00:16:21,938
- How's it going?
- Just about to start.

463
00:16:21,939 --> 00:16:23,106
- Securing the art line here.

464
00:16:23,107 --> 00:16:25,400
Measure twice, cut once.

465
00:16:25,401 --> 00:16:29,154
We enter 11 centimeters
back from the nasion,

466
00:16:29,155 --> 00:16:30,780
3 centimeters lateral.

467
00:16:30,781 --> 00:16:32,407
- Without any direct imaging?

468
00:16:32,408 --> 00:16:35,452
- No, straight shot
into the lateral ventricle--

469
00:16:35,453 --> 00:16:38,997
tried and true since 1890.

470
00:16:38,998 --> 00:16:41,334
OK, go ahead with the incision.

471
00:16:42,835 --> 00:16:45,086
- Hey, you worked
with Dr. Al-Hashimi at the VA.

472
00:16:45,087 --> 00:16:46,171
- I did.

473
00:16:46,172 --> 00:16:47,672
- For how long?

474
00:16:47,673 --> 00:16:49,633
- During my R2 rotation

475
00:16:49,634 --> 00:16:51,844
and then one again last year.

476
00:16:55,181 --> 00:16:58,350
- OK. Hand drill's all set.

477
00:16:58,351 --> 00:16:59,809
- Maybe you should do
this part.

478
00:16:59,810 --> 00:17:01,144
- No, no, there's
a safety stop here.

479
00:17:01,145 --> 00:17:02,646
You can't go too deep.

480
00:17:02,647 --> 00:17:04,690
It's not brain surgery.

481
00:17:06,859 --> 00:17:08,110
OK.

482
00:17:11,322 --> 00:17:13,156
Stay perpendicular.
- OK.

483
00:17:13,157 --> 00:17:15,075
- Good.

484
00:17:15,076 --> 00:17:16,826
All right, now, four turns

485
00:17:16,827 --> 00:17:18,036
to get through
the outer table...

486
00:17:18,037 --> 00:17:19,329
- Yep.
- Then half turns

487
00:17:19,330 --> 00:17:20,998
till you penetrate the skull.

488
00:17:24,627 --> 00:17:26,921
- [exhaling slowly]

489
00:17:31,801 --> 00:17:35,470
Three, four.

490
00:17:35,471 --> 00:17:36,722
One...

491
00:17:38,683 --> 00:17:40,350
- Holy smoke!

492
00:17:40,351 --> 00:17:43,812
I thought it was Tom Cruise
for a second over here.

493
00:17:43,813 --> 00:17:46,856
Nice job.
How's that feel, Digby?

494
00:17:46,857 --> 00:17:48,400
- Pretty good.
- I bet.

495
00:17:48,401 --> 00:17:49,693
What do you say we trim up

496
00:17:49,694 --> 00:17:51,529
those gorgeous locks
of yours now?

497
00:17:53,114 --> 00:17:55,156
- I don't like having
my hair cut.

498
00:17:55,157 --> 00:17:58,535
- I get it, Rapunzel,
but you need your ears lowered.

499
00:17:58,536 --> 00:18:00,620
You'll feel better
with a fresh summer look

500
00:18:00,621 --> 00:18:02,665
that won't go unnoticed
by the ladies.

501
00:18:04,542 --> 00:18:06,918
- Maybe just a trim?
- Great.

502
00:18:06,919 --> 00:18:09,129
I'm thinking a pixie cut.

503
00:18:09,130 --> 00:18:10,922
- What?
- I'm kidding.

504
00:18:10,923 --> 00:18:12,716
I cut my husband Benji's hair
all the time.

505
00:18:12,717 --> 00:18:14,217
Trust me, you're gonna look
fabulous.

506
00:18:14,218 --> 00:18:15,886
When's the last time
you had a haircut?

507
00:18:17,430 --> 00:18:20,724
- Maybe my daughter's wedding
a few years ago.

508
00:18:20,725 --> 00:18:22,058
- Where?

509
00:18:22,059 --> 00:18:23,268
- St. Sebastian,

510
00:18:23,269 --> 00:18:25,270
Our Lady of Mount Carmel
Parish.

511
00:18:25,271 --> 00:18:27,897
- Does your daughter know
where you are?

512
00:18:27,898 --> 00:18:31,067
- Of course.
We all live in Pittsburgh.

513
00:18:31,068 --> 00:18:33,321
In fact,
they live in my old house.

514
00:18:35,906 --> 00:18:38,241
- Your daughter lives
in your house?

515
00:18:38,242 --> 00:18:40,118
- Yep.

516
00:18:40,119 --> 00:18:42,704
- Where do you live?

517
00:18:42,705 --> 00:18:44,915
- Wherever I want.

518
00:18:46,626 --> 00:18:48,752
- You're gonna pass it
5 to 6 centimeters

519
00:18:48,753 --> 00:18:51,297
from the inner table
of the skull, OK?

520
00:18:53,257 --> 00:18:55,425
Centered...

521
00:18:55,426 --> 00:18:57,260
perpendicular...

522
00:18:57,261 --> 00:18:59,096
aiming for the medial canthus.

523
00:19:00,181 --> 00:19:01,848
All yours.

524
00:19:01,849 --> 00:19:03,183
- A-and just keep going?

525
00:19:03,184 --> 00:19:04,643
- You'll feel a pop
when you pass

526
00:19:04,644 --> 00:19:07,270
the ependymal lining.

527
00:19:07,271 --> 00:19:09,022
- Art line is calibrated.
Good to go.

528
00:19:09,023 --> 00:19:12,942
- Titrate the nicardipine
to a MAP of 85, please.

529
00:19:12,943 --> 00:19:14,361
- And if we can get
the intracranial pressure

530
00:19:14,362 --> 00:19:16,197
down to 20?

531
00:19:17,615 --> 00:19:19,032
Dr. Mohan?

532
00:19:19,033 --> 00:19:20,492
- I'm sorry.

533
00:19:20,493 --> 00:19:22,786
- MAP of 85, ICP of 20?

534
00:19:22,787 --> 00:19:25,789
- Um, cerebral perfusion
pressure 65.

535
00:19:25,790 --> 00:19:28,583
- Which gives him
the best shot at survival.

536
00:19:28,584 --> 00:19:30,085
A third of patients
like this die.

537
00:19:30,086 --> 00:19:32,629
Half have long-term disability.

538
00:19:32,630 --> 00:19:34,631
We'll know in two weeks
if there's a possibility

539
00:19:34,632 --> 00:19:36,966
for a good prognosis.

540
00:19:36,967 --> 00:19:38,134
- Felt a pop.

541
00:19:38,135 --> 00:19:39,804
- Remove the stylet.

542
00:19:42,306 --> 00:19:45,059
OK, let it drain a bit.

543
00:19:46,977 --> 00:19:49,230
And now pinch it shut.

544
00:19:50,690 --> 00:19:51,690
Perfect.

545
00:19:51,691 --> 00:19:53,608
- BP is down, 190 over 88.

546
00:19:53,609 --> 00:19:54,985
Heart rate's 67.

547
00:19:59,031 --> 00:20:00,990
- How are you guys doing?

548
00:20:00,991 --> 00:20:02,909
- Yeah, making some progress.

549
00:20:02,910 --> 00:20:04,244
- You seen Abbot?

550
00:20:04,245 --> 00:20:06,037
- Not since rounds.

551
00:20:06,038 --> 00:20:08,748
- He asked me to see a patient
with the night intern.

552
00:20:08,749 --> 00:20:10,375
- [yawning] And you said yes?

553
00:20:10,376 --> 00:20:13,253
- 48-year-old woman,
new onset jaundice,

554
00:20:13,254 --> 00:20:16,339
elevated liver enzymes,
no history of alcohol use.

555
00:20:16,340 --> 00:20:17,674
- Take your pick--

556
00:20:17,675 --> 00:20:20,385
hepatitis A, B, C, D, or E.

557
00:20:20,386 --> 00:20:23,263
- No risk factors, no fever.
- Tylenol?

558
00:20:23,264 --> 00:20:25,181
- Never,
and no prescription meds.

559
00:20:25,182 --> 00:20:27,393
- Does she eat
polar bear liver?

560
00:20:28,561 --> 00:20:30,855
It can cause
vitamin A toxicity.

561
00:20:31,731 --> 00:20:32,814
- No.
- Yeah.

562
00:20:32,815 --> 00:20:34,065
- She, uh, doesn't eat meat,

563
00:20:34,066 --> 00:20:35,692
kind of a health nut.

564
00:20:35,693 --> 00:20:37,152
- Well, maybe
she's gulping down

565
00:20:37,153 --> 00:20:38,737
cod liver oil
to prevent measles.

566
00:20:38,738 --> 00:20:40,989
- No. Vegan, no fish.

567
00:20:40,990 --> 00:20:43,032
Would you, uh, mind
laying eyes on her?

568
00:20:43,033 --> 00:20:44,659
- [sighs] Seriously?

569
00:20:44,660 --> 00:20:46,995
- You have a way of
figuring out the weird stuff.

570
00:20:46,996 --> 00:20:48,539
- Take the compliment.

571
00:20:49,874 --> 00:20:51,249
- OK, fine.

572
00:20:51,250 --> 00:20:53,543
Five minutes at the bedside--
that's it.

573
00:20:53,544 --> 00:20:55,545
I'm already gonna be here
for hours as it is.

574
00:20:55,546 --> 00:20:57,756
- Thank you.

575
00:20:57,757 --> 00:20:59,341
- What am I doing--

576
00:21:02,553 --> 00:21:04,888
- Definitely improving.
- Tidal volume's up.

577
00:21:04,889 --> 00:21:07,390
- Wheezes now expiratory only.

578
00:21:07,391 --> 00:21:08,975
- Oh, making some progress.

579
00:21:08,976 --> 00:21:11,102
- You feeling better, baby?

580
00:21:11,103 --> 00:21:14,397
You scared the shit out of me.
- Language!

581
00:21:14,398 --> 00:21:16,733
- Does he have
a primary-care doctor?

582
00:21:16,734 --> 00:21:18,109
- Not anymore.

583
00:21:18,110 --> 00:21:20,737
- How do you get
his prescriptions renewed?

584
00:21:20,738 --> 00:21:22,906
- Neighborhood clinic.
We pay cash.

585
00:21:22,907 --> 00:21:24,741
Albuterol's cheap.
- Symbicort?

586
00:21:24,742 --> 00:21:27,660
- Without Medicaid,
400 bucks a month.

587
00:21:27,661 --> 00:21:29,163
- Just for a little inhaler?

588
00:21:31,248 --> 00:21:33,750
- Trocar is on the tube.
[inhales deeply]

589
00:21:33,751 --> 00:21:37,128
I tunnel underneath the scalp
about 5 centimeters

590
00:21:37,129 --> 00:21:38,630
and poke through.

591
00:21:38,631 --> 00:21:40,424
- To prevent infection?
- Mm-hmm, exactly.

592
00:21:41,884 --> 00:21:44,512
Now you hold the knuckle steady
while I pull it through.

593
00:21:47,807 --> 00:21:49,975
And you can staple
the wound shut.

594
00:21:51,435 --> 00:21:53,603
- That looks like something
from Home Depot.

595
00:21:53,604 --> 00:21:55,438
- Yeah, big
Fourth of July sale.

596
00:21:55,439 --> 00:21:56,773
- The brain pressure
is measured by

597
00:21:56,774 --> 00:21:58,608
the height of the fluid column.

598
00:21:58,609 --> 00:22:01,152
We need to calibrate it to zero
at the level of the tragus.

599
00:22:01,153 --> 00:22:03,196
- Stopcock's on,
ready to attach.

600
00:22:03,197 --> 00:22:04,614
- First troponin's normal,

601
00:22:04,615 --> 00:22:07,033
but you need a second
to rule out MI.

602
00:22:07,034 --> 00:22:08,869
- Hey, can you step out
for a second?

603
00:22:15,000 --> 00:22:16,459
- I really did do
everything I could

604
00:22:16,460 --> 00:22:18,044
to try to make Orlando stay.

605
00:22:18,045 --> 00:22:19,045
- Yeah, I don't doubt it.

606
00:22:19,046 --> 00:22:20,046
I wanted to ask you about

607
00:22:20,047 --> 00:22:22,257
Dr. Al-Hashimi at the VA.

608
00:22:22,258 --> 00:22:24,968
Did you ever notice anything
unusual about her behavior?

609
00:22:24,969 --> 00:22:26,970
- Um, like what?

610
00:22:26,971 --> 00:22:29,597
- Lapses in focus, attention?

611
00:22:29,598 --> 00:22:32,184
- No, she was always
on top of her game.

612
00:22:35,145 --> 00:22:38,815
But this morning when she was
checking labs on Baby Jane Doe,

613
00:22:38,816 --> 00:22:41,193
it was like she zoned out
for a few seconds.

614
00:22:42,653 --> 00:22:44,488
Maybe she was just tired.

615
00:22:46,448 --> 00:22:49,117
- [inhales deeply]

616
00:22:49,118 --> 00:22:50,578
[sighs]

617
00:22:51,495 --> 00:22:53,830
- No congestion, nodules,

618
00:22:53,831 --> 00:22:55,290
or cirrhosis.

619
00:22:55,291 --> 00:22:56,958
- Good-looking liver.

620
00:22:56,959 --> 00:22:58,835
- I should not be sick.

621
00:22:58,836 --> 00:23:01,462
I work so hard
to stay healthy--

622
00:23:01,463 --> 00:23:04,340
diet, exercise,
eight hours of sleep.

623
00:23:04,341 --> 00:23:08,177
- I can't remember the last
time I got eight hours.

624
00:23:08,178 --> 00:23:10,179
- Maybe you should try
sleepmaxxing.

625
00:23:10,180 --> 00:23:12,098
- Oh, you definitely should.

626
00:23:12,099 --> 00:23:15,018
- Yeah, cold room,
white noise, eye shades.

627
00:23:15,019 --> 00:23:18,855
- Kiwis before bedtime,
lettuce water, mouth taping.

628
00:23:18,856 --> 00:23:20,523
- Who do you follow for health?

629
00:23:20,524 --> 00:23:22,859
- A few naturopaths
and some functional healers.

630
00:23:22,860 --> 00:23:24,861
- Do you--do you buy
vitamins from them?

631
00:23:24,862 --> 00:23:26,654
- Oh, no, no,
I don't take vitamins.

632
00:23:26,655 --> 00:23:29,449
Food is the best medicine--
kale, blueberries,

633
00:23:29,450 --> 00:23:32,035
kidney beans, turmeric.
- Turmeric?

634
00:23:32,036 --> 00:23:33,745
- Uh, how long have you
been taking that?

635
00:23:33,746 --> 00:23:36,331
- For a few months.
It's an anti-inflammatory.

636
00:23:36,332 --> 00:23:38,875
It detoxes the body,
and it can prevent Alzheimer's.

637
00:23:38,876 --> 00:23:40,335
- How much do you take?

638
00:23:40,336 --> 00:23:42,337
- 500 milligram capsules,

639
00:23:42,338 --> 00:23:43,589
but I take five a day.

640
00:23:45,257 --> 00:23:46,883
What?

641
00:23:46,884 --> 00:23:48,718
- With doses that large
of turmeric,

642
00:23:48,719 --> 00:23:51,346
there have been cases
of liver failure.

643
00:23:51,347 --> 00:23:53,056
- From eating a spice?

644
00:23:53,057 --> 00:23:55,725
- All right, Digby,
I think you're good to go.

645
00:23:55,726 --> 00:23:57,811
- Mm-hmm.
Would you like to see?

646
00:24:08,155 --> 00:24:09,406
- [sniffles]

647
00:24:10,407 --> 00:24:12,909
I haven't looked like this
in a long time.

648
00:24:12,910 --> 00:24:15,329
- Your family
won't recognize you.

649
00:24:17,247 --> 00:24:19,415
- Then how will they find me?

650
00:24:19,416 --> 00:24:21,417
They--they won't know
what I look like.

651
00:24:21,418 --> 00:24:22,460
They won't know this is me.

652
00:24:22,461 --> 00:24:23,586
- Of course they will.

653
00:24:23,587 --> 00:24:24,837
They know your voice.

654
00:24:24,838 --> 00:24:26,673
They know where you hang out.

655
00:24:26,674 --> 00:24:30,510
They even remember that
you used to look like this.

656
00:24:30,511 --> 00:24:32,554
They remember the wedding.

657
00:24:35,474 --> 00:24:37,767
- Did you dance
with your daughter

658
00:24:37,768 --> 00:24:39,186
at her wedding?

659
00:24:40,604 --> 00:24:42,772
- I did.

660
00:24:42,773 --> 00:24:45,859
- Then she will always
remember you.

661
00:24:48,570 --> 00:24:50,114
- Yeah.
- OK.

662
00:24:51,615 --> 00:24:53,241
- Hey, I was just
looking for you.

663
00:24:53,242 --> 00:24:54,575
- What's up?
- You tell me.

664
00:24:54,576 --> 00:24:56,911
The, uh, asthma patient.
- What about him?

665
00:24:56,912 --> 00:24:58,287
- Seemed like
you were hesitating

666
00:24:58,288 --> 00:24:59,956
talking about using Aerogen.

667
00:24:59,957 --> 00:25:01,624
I was wondering if you were
having second thoughts.

668
00:25:01,625 --> 00:25:03,418
- No. Just thinking.

669
00:25:03,419 --> 00:25:04,669
- About?

670
00:25:04,670 --> 00:25:06,671
- The best treatment plan
for the patient.

671
00:25:06,672 --> 00:25:08,798
And I think he's on it.

672
00:25:08,799 --> 00:25:10,299
Anything else?
- I don't know.

673
00:25:10,300 --> 00:25:12,844
You tell me.
Anything else I need to know?

674
00:25:12,845 --> 00:25:14,179
- I don't think so.

675
00:25:15,806 --> 00:25:17,306
- This is Dr. Robby in the ED.

676
00:25:17,307 --> 00:25:19,017
I'm checking on a patient,
Ekins.

677
00:25:19,018 --> 00:25:21,561
- We can take Larson
and Stevens off the dry-erase.

678
00:25:21,562 --> 00:25:22,645
- Consider it done.

679
00:25:22,646 --> 00:25:23,813
- He's in the scanner now?

680
00:25:23,814 --> 00:25:25,273
Oh, great.

681
00:25:25,274 --> 00:25:26,357
No, I was just calling
to confirm.

682
00:25:26,358 --> 00:25:27,650
Thank you.

683
00:25:27,651 --> 00:25:29,485
Any new information
from upstairs?

684
00:25:29,486 --> 00:25:30,903
- Nothing we don't
already know.

685
00:25:30,904 --> 00:25:32,572
- Same old story, huh?

686
00:25:32,573 --> 00:25:33,990
- You think we should
take up a collection

687
00:25:33,991 --> 00:25:35,825
for Jesse for--for bail money?

688
00:25:35,826 --> 00:25:37,368
- They don't usually set bail

689
00:25:37,369 --> 00:25:39,162
until after you've appeared
in front of a judge.

690
00:25:39,163 --> 00:25:41,039
- With the holiday, it's not
likely to happen till Monday.

691
00:25:41,040 --> 00:25:43,207
- So he's gonna be locked up
all weekend?

692
00:25:43,208 --> 00:25:44,959
- That sucks.

693
00:25:44,960 --> 00:25:46,961
- It does suck.

694
00:25:46,962 --> 00:25:49,964
- Dr. Langdon, Grady's worse--
much worse.

695
00:25:49,965 --> 00:25:52,800
- Need an attending?
- Uh, no, we have Shen.

696
00:25:52,801 --> 00:25:54,177
- Sat's down to 83.

697
00:25:54,178 --> 00:25:56,304
- What's happening?
- Give us a minute.

698
00:25:56,305 --> 00:25:57,805
- He's tiring out.

699
00:25:57,806 --> 00:25:59,849
Ketamine and rock.
It's time to intubate.

700
00:25:59,850 --> 00:26:01,017
- I'll call Volk.

701
00:26:01,018 --> 00:26:02,810
- Ma'am, we have to put a tube

702
00:26:02,811 --> 00:26:04,020
down your son's windpipe

703
00:26:04,021 --> 00:26:05,521
so a machine
can breathe for him.

704
00:26:05,522 --> 00:26:06,856
- [crying] Oh, my God.

705
00:26:06,857 --> 00:26:08,357
- Hold on, hold on.

706
00:26:08,358 --> 00:26:09,567
No lung sliding on the right.

707
00:26:09,568 --> 00:26:11,069
- Pneumothorax.

708
00:26:11,070 --> 00:26:13,112
- No need to intubate.

709
00:26:13,113 --> 00:26:14,405
- What's going on?

710
00:26:14,406 --> 00:26:16,365
- He has a collapsed lung.

711
00:26:16,366 --> 00:26:18,284
- There was no history
of trauma

712
00:26:18,285 --> 00:26:19,786
that would have caused
pneumothorax.

713
00:26:19,787 --> 00:26:21,871
- Glove up, Dr. King.

714
00:26:21,872 --> 00:26:24,207
- In asthma, you can get
"air trapping."

715
00:26:24,208 --> 00:26:25,917
When the lungs can't
fully exhale,

716
00:26:25,918 --> 00:26:27,293
eventually they overinflate,

717
00:26:27,294 --> 00:26:29,212
causing some of the tiny
air sacs to burst.

718
00:26:29,213 --> 00:26:30,421
- How do you fix it?

719
00:26:30,422 --> 00:26:33,467
[rapid beeping]

720
00:26:34,635 --> 00:26:36,302
[air whooshing]
- [gasping]

721
00:26:36,303 --> 00:26:37,346
- Like that.

722
00:26:42,935 --> 00:26:45,561
- It's a good thing
Dr. Conley was here.

723
00:26:45,562 --> 00:26:46,896
- Yeah.
- We should probably try

724
00:26:46,897 --> 00:26:48,397
to find him
a Neuro-Critical ICU bed.

725
00:26:48,398 --> 00:26:50,024
We don't want to board
a patient like this.

726
00:26:50,025 --> 00:26:51,692
- Mm-hmm.
- Hey, Robby.

727
00:26:51,693 --> 00:26:54,237
- Hey, how did the scan go?
- Fine.

728
00:26:54,238 --> 00:26:55,738
- It is probably gonna take
a little while

729
00:26:55,739 --> 00:26:57,198
to get the results
from the radiologist.

730
00:26:57,199 --> 00:26:58,616
- Well, that's not a problem.

731
00:26:58,617 --> 00:27:00,868
Nurse Vivi and I need
a little time

732
00:27:00,869 --> 00:27:03,412
to coordinate our schedules
over the next few months.

733
00:27:03,413 --> 00:27:04,413
- Really?
- Yeah.

734
00:27:04,414 --> 00:27:05,414
I've always wanted to learn

735
00:27:05,415 --> 00:27:06,457
how to ride a motorcycle,

736
00:27:06,458 --> 00:27:07,959
and Duke offered to teach me.

737
00:27:07,960 --> 00:27:10,253
- Well, nobody knows
motorcycles like Duke.

738
00:27:10,254 --> 00:27:12,713
- He's giving me the health
care worker discount.

739
00:27:12,714 --> 00:27:14,006
- He's quite the gentleman.

740
00:27:14,007 --> 00:27:15,800
Let's get him back to his room.

741
00:27:15,801 --> 00:27:18,594
- How would next Saturday be
for you, Vivi?

742
00:27:18,595 --> 00:27:21,931
Uh, maybe we could have
some dinner after.

743
00:27:21,932 --> 00:27:23,558
- Dr. Mohan,
Lorrie Diaz is here.

744
00:27:23,559 --> 00:27:25,309
She wants to see her husband.
She's heading back.

745
00:27:25,310 --> 00:27:26,854
- OK, I'm on my way.

746
00:27:28,981 --> 00:27:30,982
Mrs. Diaz?
- Hi.

747
00:27:30,983 --> 00:27:33,860
Did Orlando go up
to his room yet?

748
00:27:33,861 --> 00:27:35,361
- No. Um--

749
00:27:35,362 --> 00:27:37,488
- I brought him some dinner
from Burgatory.

750
00:27:37,489 --> 00:27:38,865
It's a chicken burger--

751
00:27:38,866 --> 00:27:40,491
no bun, just lettuce.

752
00:27:40,492 --> 00:27:43,203
Figured it would be better
than hospital food.

753
00:27:46,456 --> 00:27:50,751
- He's being treated by our top
brain-injury specialists.

754
00:27:50,752 --> 00:27:52,253
- Will he wake up?

755
00:27:52,254 --> 00:27:54,130
- We won't know
for several weeks.

756
00:27:54,131 --> 00:27:56,757
- And then he could be
back to normal?

757
00:27:56,758 --> 00:27:59,844
- Best-case scenario,
it would take about a year

758
00:27:59,845 --> 00:28:02,471
before he could be
independent at home.

759
00:28:02,472 --> 00:28:04,140
- And the worst case?

760
00:28:04,141 --> 00:28:07,644
- Round-the-clock nursing care
for severe disability.

761
00:28:11,190 --> 00:28:13,441
- You let him leave?

762
00:28:13,442 --> 00:28:17,403
- He insisted on leaving
to work at his second job.

763
00:28:17,404 --> 00:28:19,655
- You couldn't make him stay?

764
00:28:19,656 --> 00:28:22,658
- He was competent.
He knew the risks.

765
00:28:22,659 --> 00:28:24,660
We honored his decision
to leave.

766
00:28:24,661 --> 00:28:26,412
- Even if it was a stupid one?

767
00:28:26,413 --> 00:28:27,914
- I tried to stop him.

768
00:28:27,915 --> 00:28:29,540
Offered to get him
all the supplies he'd need

769
00:28:29,541 --> 00:28:31,042
to take care of himself
at home.

770
00:28:31,043 --> 00:28:33,586
But he left before
I could give them to him.

771
00:28:33,587 --> 00:28:36,048
He said he couldn't afford
to lose a paycheck.

772
00:28:43,555 --> 00:28:45,848
- Dr. Langdon.

773
00:28:45,849 --> 00:28:46,850
[door closes]

774
00:28:48,018 --> 00:28:49,727
Are you OK?

775
00:28:49,728 --> 00:28:52,855
- Yeah. I'm just kind of, uh...

776
00:28:52,856 --> 00:28:55,358
wondering if I'm really
ready to be back here.

777
00:28:55,359 --> 00:28:57,652
- Well, of course you are.

778
00:28:57,653 --> 00:28:59,737
- Ten months is a long time.

779
00:28:59,738 --> 00:29:03,032
I almost killed that kid
with an intubation.

780
00:29:03,033 --> 00:29:06,327
Didn't even occur to me
to check for pneumothorax.

781
00:29:06,328 --> 00:29:08,037
- There wasn't any trauma
to indicate--

782
00:29:08,038 --> 00:29:09,206
- I should have caught it.
- OK.

783
00:29:12,000 --> 00:29:13,627
- [sighs]

784
00:29:15,879 --> 00:29:17,255
- You know, I, um...

785
00:29:17,256 --> 00:29:19,090
[chair scraping]

786
00:29:19,091 --> 00:29:22,009
I had a deposition today,

787
00:29:22,010 --> 00:29:25,471
and all the lawyer's questions
made me feel like

788
00:29:25,472 --> 00:29:27,808
I was a really,
really bad doctor.

789
00:29:30,143 --> 00:29:33,689
We don't always get everything
right the first time.

790
00:29:34,773 --> 00:29:37,316
You would have caught
the collapsed lung.

791
00:29:37,317 --> 00:29:40,028
And it may have taken a minute,
but you would have saved him.

792
00:29:41,238 --> 00:29:43,614
- I'm not sure
Robby would agree with you.

793
00:29:43,615 --> 00:29:46,325
He's been riding me all day.

794
00:29:46,326 --> 00:29:48,244
- Well, Robby's leaving
for three months.

795
00:29:48,245 --> 00:29:51,080
And, you know,
with Robby gone...

796
00:29:51,081 --> 00:29:53,083
I really don't want you
to leave either.

797
00:29:56,586 --> 00:29:59,255
That which does not kill me
makes me stronger.

798
00:29:59,256 --> 00:30:02,550
- Wow, Friedrich Nietzsche.

799
00:30:02,551 --> 00:30:05,761
- Yeah, Nietzsche, yeah...

800
00:30:05,762 --> 00:30:08,097
not Kelly Clarkson.

801
00:30:08,098 --> 00:30:10,057
- [chuckles]

802
00:30:10,058 --> 00:30:12,059
- Hey, that's a thick one.

803
00:30:12,060 --> 00:30:14,937
- Yeah, Austin Green.
Ogilvie's triple-A.

804
00:30:14,938 --> 00:30:17,940
[beeping, whirring]

805
00:30:17,941 --> 00:30:20,526
- Uh, G-R-E-E-N?

806
00:30:20,527 --> 00:30:21,944
- Yeah. Why?
What are you doing?

807
00:30:21,945 --> 00:30:23,446
- Checking to see
when we'll get Ogilvie

808
00:30:23,447 --> 00:30:25,323
back from the OR.

809
00:30:25,324 --> 00:30:27,366
Oh, shit.
- What?

810
00:30:27,367 --> 00:30:29,118
- The guy didn't make it.

811
00:30:29,119 --> 00:30:31,454
He died on the table
about 40 minutes ago.

812
00:30:31,455 --> 00:30:33,622
- Then where's Ogilvie?

813
00:30:33,623 --> 00:30:35,541
- Oh, man.

814
00:30:35,542 --> 00:30:38,169
His patient had a CT
a year and a half ago

815
00:30:38,170 --> 00:30:40,463
that showed
a 4-centimeter triple-A.

816
00:30:40,464 --> 00:30:42,131
- That's too small for surgery.

817
00:30:42,132 --> 00:30:43,466
- He was supposed
to come back

818
00:30:43,467 --> 00:30:45,217
every six months
for a repeat ultrasound,

819
00:30:45,218 --> 00:30:46,927
but looks like he didn't.

820
00:30:46,928 --> 00:30:49,638
- We would've seen that
if the computers were working.

821
00:30:49,639 --> 00:30:51,307
Why wouldn't he tell Ogilvie?

822
00:30:51,308 --> 00:30:53,142
- [sighing] I don't know.
Maybe he forgot all about it.

823
00:30:53,143 --> 00:30:55,187
Or Ogilvie didn't ask
the right questions.

824
00:30:56,271 --> 00:30:58,314
- Dennis, hey, got a minute?

825
00:30:58,315 --> 00:31:00,816
- Uh, sure. What do you need?

826
00:31:00,817 --> 00:31:02,485
- Teenager with jewelry magnets

827
00:31:02,486 --> 00:31:04,487
stuck to both sides
of her nasal septum.

828
00:31:04,488 --> 00:31:06,489
I tried to move them
with a plastic curette.

829
00:31:06,490 --> 00:31:08,324
Too much pain,
and then there was bleeding.

830
00:31:08,325 --> 00:31:10,659
- Those need to come off before
they cause permanent damage.

831
00:31:10,660 --> 00:31:12,203
- Yeah, you think, uh,
you could find, like,

832
00:31:12,204 --> 00:31:14,121
a senior or an attending?

833
00:31:14,122 --> 00:31:15,998
We're really trying
to get off the clock here.

834
00:31:15,999 --> 00:31:17,167
- They're all busy.

835
00:31:19,836 --> 00:31:21,504
- I already took one
for the team.

836
00:31:21,505 --> 00:31:23,422
- Yeah, fine.

837
00:31:23,423 --> 00:31:27,009
- Uh, labs on your yellow lady.

838
00:31:27,010 --> 00:31:29,428
- INR is elevated, 2.2.

839
00:31:29,429 --> 00:31:31,389
- Liver's in trouble
but not dead yet.

840
00:31:31,390 --> 00:31:32,973
- Tell her to skip
the turmeric.

841
00:31:32,974 --> 00:31:35,184
Admit for monitoring,
and follow the LFTs.

842
00:31:35,185 --> 00:31:36,352
- Got it.
- Mm-hmm.

843
00:31:36,353 --> 00:31:37,813
- Central 10?
- Yeah.

844
00:31:42,192 --> 00:31:44,360
- "Sleepmaxxing"? Really?

845
00:31:44,361 --> 00:31:45,903
- I track the online trends.

846
00:31:45,904 --> 00:31:48,280
- Is that your secret
for the tough ones?

847
00:31:48,281 --> 00:31:49,990
- I just think,

848
00:31:49,991 --> 00:31:52,368
what's the stupidest thing
this person could have done?

849
00:31:52,369 --> 00:31:53,620
Then assume they did it.

850
00:31:55,038 --> 00:31:57,206
- Well, I mean,
she was trying to be healthy.

851
00:31:57,207 --> 00:31:59,542
- Yeah, by taking advice
from fools.

852
00:31:59,543 --> 00:32:01,752
- Turmeric is recommended
by a lot of doctors.

853
00:32:01,753 --> 00:32:03,754
- Yeah, then ask your doctor
for the dose

854
00:32:03,755 --> 00:32:06,382
or check someplace reputable,
like the CDC.

855
00:32:06,383 --> 00:32:09,677
Oh, right, I forgot, it's now
a medical toxic waste site.

856
00:32:09,678 --> 00:32:12,680
- I mean,
she's in pretty bad shape.

857
00:32:12,681 --> 00:32:15,015
If things get worse, she could
need a liver transplant.

858
00:32:15,016 --> 00:32:17,102
- Yeah, 'cause she's a big
fucking idiot.

859
00:32:18,603 --> 00:32:20,438
- Wow.

860
00:32:20,439 --> 00:32:22,690
- No, I actually
appreciate her.

861
00:32:22,691 --> 00:32:25,444
Our job security depends on
all these big fucking idiots.

862
00:32:27,821 --> 00:32:29,072
- OK.

863
00:32:31,408 --> 00:32:34,827
- Uh, good night,
Perlah, Monica.

864
00:32:34,828 --> 00:32:37,580
Good night, Dr. Robby.
- Good night.

865
00:32:37,581 --> 00:32:39,165
- Bye, Emma.

866
00:32:39,166 --> 00:32:40,624
- You could stay to pitch in.

867
00:32:40,625 --> 00:32:42,585
- Mm, Dana told me
to go and get some rest

868
00:32:42,586 --> 00:32:44,003
before tomorrow's shift.

869
00:32:44,004 --> 00:32:45,671
- Yeah, you had quite a day.

870
00:32:45,672 --> 00:32:47,048
- Wasn't so bad.

871
00:32:49,009 --> 00:32:50,843
- Why can't I find
Duke's results?

872
00:32:50,844 --> 00:32:53,596
- Oh, new patient, no EHR yet.

873
00:32:53,597 --> 00:32:56,432
- I can run over to Radiology,
get a printout.

874
00:32:56,433 --> 00:32:57,809
- Thank you, Monica.

875
00:33:00,312 --> 00:33:02,313
- Ready to pick up a few more.

876
00:33:02,314 --> 00:33:03,772
Orlando's all tucked in
for now.

877
00:33:03,773 --> 00:33:05,399
- How's he doing?
- Numbers are good.

878
00:33:05,400 --> 00:33:07,234
CPP 22.

879
00:33:07,235 --> 00:33:09,820
- OK, so he's got a shot
at a decent outcome at least.

880
00:33:09,821 --> 00:33:11,447
- You think he has a chance?

881
00:33:11,448 --> 00:33:13,657
- Oh, I don't know
that survival was

882
00:33:13,658 --> 00:33:15,659
the outcome he was hoping for.

883
00:33:15,660 --> 00:33:17,286
- What?

884
00:33:17,287 --> 00:33:19,497
- He told Samira he's got
$100,000 in medical debt,

885
00:33:19,498 --> 00:33:21,958
that his life is probably--
- Mrs. Diaz!

886
00:33:24,294 --> 00:33:26,754
How can we help you?
- The bathroom?

887
00:33:26,755 --> 00:33:29,424
- Uh, yeah.
Perlah can show you the way.

888
00:33:31,176 --> 00:33:33,636
Not a great idea to have
a private conversation

889
00:33:33,637 --> 00:33:34,845
about a patient
in a public area.

890
00:33:34,846 --> 00:33:36,847
- I know. I know.

891
00:33:36,848 --> 00:33:38,224
- You were
about to start yapping--

892
00:33:38,225 --> 00:33:39,768
- But I didn't!

893
00:33:42,312 --> 00:33:45,231
- OK.

894
00:33:45,232 --> 00:33:47,566
And how long have they
been in there?

895
00:33:47,567 --> 00:33:49,026
- At least two hours.

896
00:33:49,027 --> 00:33:51,487
- How did it happen?
- With my earring.

897
00:33:51,488 --> 00:33:54,365
It was supposed to be, uh,
red, white, and blue

898
00:33:54,366 --> 00:33:56,158
to look cool at the fireworks.

899
00:33:56,159 --> 00:33:57,868
- I told her for the nose,
one side only,

900
00:33:57,869 --> 00:33:59,161
but she never listens.

901
00:33:59,162 --> 00:34:00,371
- This can be pretty serious.

902
00:34:00,372 --> 00:34:01,664
If we don't get them out
quickly,

903
00:34:01,665 --> 00:34:03,123
it can cut off the blood supply

904
00:34:03,124 --> 00:34:05,000
and cause the septum
to collapse.

905
00:34:05,001 --> 00:34:08,588
- Like, my nose
could be deformed?

906
00:34:10,799 --> 00:34:13,384
Oh, my God, get them off.
- Yeah, that's the plan.

907
00:34:13,385 --> 00:34:15,135
- Yeah, she tried already.
It hurt like crazy.

908
00:34:15,136 --> 00:34:16,303
- Because they're
stuck together

909
00:34:16,304 --> 00:34:18,389
by a very strong
magnetic force.

910
00:34:18,390 --> 00:34:20,349
- Yeah.
- No, no, no.

911
00:34:20,350 --> 00:34:22,059
- Oh, uh, I'm not gonna
use the tips.

912
00:34:22,060 --> 00:34:25,145
I'm gonna flip it around
and use the handle.

913
00:34:25,146 --> 00:34:26,730
- It's still gonna hurt.

914
00:34:26,731 --> 00:34:27,982
- Probably not.

915
00:34:27,983 --> 00:34:29,317
Just, uh, hold still.

916
00:34:31,861 --> 00:34:33,280
OK.

917
00:34:36,533 --> 00:34:38,784
[sighs] Voilà.

918
00:34:38,785 --> 00:34:41,537
- Really?
- That was amazing.

919
00:34:41,538 --> 00:34:43,372
- I think another half hour,

920
00:34:43,373 --> 00:34:45,040
we can send this dry-erase
board back to storage.

921
00:34:45,041 --> 00:34:46,250
- With pleasure.

922
00:34:46,251 --> 00:34:47,626
- Dr. Robinavitch?
- Yep.

923
00:34:47,627 --> 00:34:49,087
- Results on your friend.

924
00:34:53,717 --> 00:34:55,927
- Fuck.
- Duke?

925
00:34:59,889 --> 00:35:01,557
Are you gonna tell him?

926
00:35:01,558 --> 00:35:03,183
- I want to talk
to a surgeon first.

927
00:35:03,184 --> 00:35:05,227
- Uh, Dr. Robby, I have
an update on the patient.

928
00:35:05,228 --> 00:35:06,687
- Now is not a great time.

929
00:35:06,688 --> 00:35:08,063
- Oh, I thought
you were going home.

930
00:35:08,064 --> 00:35:09,273
- Uh, I was.

931
00:35:09,274 --> 00:35:11,066
Dr. Ogilvie is just sitting

932
00:35:11,067 --> 00:35:12,776
out in the ambulance bay
covered in blood.

933
00:35:12,777 --> 00:35:14,403
- Sorry, what--
what's he doing out there?

934
00:35:14,404 --> 00:35:16,363
- Kind of, uh,
staring off into space.

935
00:35:16,364 --> 00:35:17,698
I tried to talk to him,
but it was like

936
00:35:17,699 --> 00:35:19,116
he couldn't hear me.
- Monica,

937
00:35:19,117 --> 00:35:20,743
can you stat page
CardioThoracic?

938
00:35:20,744 --> 00:35:22,912
- I'll go check on Ogilvie.
- Please.

939
00:35:25,206 --> 00:35:26,416
- Oh.

940
00:35:35,592 --> 00:35:37,801
[sighs]

941
00:35:37,802 --> 00:35:40,013
Hey, Ogilvie.

942
00:35:42,599 --> 00:35:44,600
Ogilvie...

943
00:35:44,601 --> 00:35:46,436
y-you OK, man?

944
00:35:49,856 --> 00:35:51,482
[sighs]

945
00:35:51,483 --> 00:35:53,609
- 99 on 2 liters.

946
00:35:53,610 --> 00:35:55,944
- Scattered end
expiratory wheezes.

947
00:35:55,945 --> 00:35:58,155
- He is so much better.

948
00:35:58,156 --> 00:36:00,199
- The steroids
should kick in soon.

949
00:36:00,200 --> 00:36:02,618
- So he'll keep improving.

950
00:36:02,619 --> 00:36:04,953
- Um, when can I go home?

951
00:36:04,954 --> 00:36:06,622
- In a day or two.

952
00:36:06,623 --> 00:36:09,917
And we will, uh, discharge you
with a Symbicort inhaler.

953
00:36:09,918 --> 00:36:12,086
That should last about a month.

954
00:36:12,087 --> 00:36:14,630
- Hopefully we'll be
back on Medicaid by then.

955
00:36:14,631 --> 00:36:16,632
- How'd you lose it?

956
00:36:16,633 --> 00:36:20,135
- They sent a redetermination
letter to our old apartment.

957
00:36:20,136 --> 00:36:22,680
It didn't get forwarded.

958
00:36:22,681 --> 00:36:24,765
When I went to pick up
his prescription,

959
00:36:24,766 --> 00:36:28,602
pharmacist told me
we were no longer covered.

960
00:36:28,603 --> 00:36:30,813
- And you haven't been able
to get it back since?

961
00:36:30,814 --> 00:36:33,148
- I've been trying for months.

962
00:36:33,149 --> 00:36:35,651
To get re-enrolled,
you need tax returns,

963
00:36:35,652 --> 00:36:37,027
pay stubs.

964
00:36:37,028 --> 00:36:39,988
I cut hair.
My income fluctuates.

965
00:36:39,989 --> 00:36:42,408
A lot of my regulars
are struggling,

966
00:36:42,409 --> 00:36:45,536
so it's been tough.

967
00:36:45,537 --> 00:36:47,496
- Well, if you run out
of Symbicort

968
00:36:47,497 --> 00:36:49,832
before your Medicaid kicks in,
you can come back,

969
00:36:49,833 --> 00:36:52,751
and we'll, uh, set you up
with another one.

970
00:36:52,752 --> 00:36:54,753
- Oh, is there
a patient in here?

971
00:36:54,754 --> 00:36:57,840
'Cause everyone in this room
looks extremely healthy to me.

972
00:36:57,841 --> 00:37:00,342
- Dr. Shen and I will be
with Grady all night long.

973
00:37:00,343 --> 00:37:02,261
- Yeah. Day shift, we got this.

974
00:37:02,262 --> 00:37:04,221
- We're leaving you
in good hands.

975
00:37:04,222 --> 00:37:06,056
- Um...

976
00:37:06,057 --> 00:37:08,517
I was really scared.

977
00:37:08,518 --> 00:37:09,686
Thank you.

978
00:37:11,354 --> 00:37:13,356
- You're very welcome.

979
00:37:17,235 --> 00:37:19,863
- Due to his injury,
things have changed.

980
00:37:21,322 --> 00:37:23,574
- I was looking for Dr. Abbot.

981
00:37:23,575 --> 00:37:26,410
- I haven't seen him
for a while.

982
00:37:26,411 --> 00:37:27,704
- Excuse me.

983
00:37:29,414 --> 00:37:30,706
[door opens]

984
00:37:30,707 --> 00:37:32,708
- So now,
with a long-term disability,

985
00:37:32,709 --> 00:37:35,419
things may be easier.

986
00:37:35,420 --> 00:37:36,587
- "Easier"?

987
00:37:36,588 --> 00:37:38,088
- His condition
will qualify him

988
00:37:38,089 --> 00:37:39,590
for Medicare and Medicaid.

989
00:37:39,591 --> 00:37:42,092
So, moving forward,
costs should be covered,

990
00:37:42,093 --> 00:37:43,886
including home health care.

991
00:37:43,887 --> 00:37:45,220
- OK.

992
00:37:45,221 --> 00:37:46,722
- Perhaps this isn't
the best time--

993
00:37:46,723 --> 00:37:48,056
- I just wanted to reassure you

994
00:37:48,057 --> 00:37:49,934
about future costs.

995
00:37:58,109 --> 00:37:59,526
- Robby.

996
00:37:59,527 --> 00:38:01,737
- Noelle.

997
00:38:01,738 --> 00:38:03,322
- You're still here.

998
00:38:03,323 --> 00:38:05,657
- It's not the best day
to try to get out on time.

999
00:38:05,658 --> 00:38:07,367
- Mm.

1000
00:38:07,368 --> 00:38:08,786
So I guess this is it
for a while.

1001
00:38:08,787 --> 00:38:10,287
- Unless you want
to come with me.

1002
00:38:10,288 --> 00:38:12,706
- No, but thank you,

1003
00:38:12,707 --> 00:38:14,750
even if it isn't
a legitimate offer.

1004
00:38:14,751 --> 00:38:16,251
- What do you mean?
- Oh, please.

1005
00:38:16,252 --> 00:38:18,086
I know well enough
not to get in between

1006
00:38:18,087 --> 00:38:20,173
a man and his vision quest.

1007
00:38:21,591 --> 00:38:22,925
- "Vision quest."

1008
00:38:22,926 --> 00:38:26,095
- That is my nice way
of putting it.

1009
00:38:27,263 --> 00:38:28,597
- I probably don't want to hear

1010
00:38:28,598 --> 00:38:30,474
your not-so-nice way
of putting it.

1011
00:38:30,475 --> 00:38:31,935
- No, probably not.

1012
00:38:33,603 --> 00:38:37,231
I-I hope that
this is not, um...

1013
00:38:37,232 --> 00:38:38,607
about you running away from me.

1014
00:38:38,608 --> 00:38:39,817
I'm a big girl.

1015
00:38:39,818 --> 00:38:41,485
You can tell me
just to get lost.

1016
00:38:41,486 --> 00:38:43,320
- This has nothing
to do with you.

1017
00:38:43,321 --> 00:38:44,613
- Oh, right.

1018
00:38:44,614 --> 00:38:47,367
It's the old, uh,
"it's not you, it's me"?

1019
00:38:48,827 --> 00:38:51,037
- In this case,
it's actually true.

1020
00:38:57,210 --> 00:38:58,878
- Excuse me.

1021
00:39:04,968 --> 00:39:06,301
- I'll see you next week?

1022
00:39:06,302 --> 00:39:07,636
- It's a three-month
sabbatical.

1023
00:39:07,637 --> 00:39:09,639
- Like I said,
I will see you next week.

1024
00:39:16,312 --> 00:39:19,606
- My first day here
as a med student...

1025
00:39:19,607 --> 00:39:21,316
there was a patient.

1026
00:39:21,317 --> 00:39:23,026
[sighs]

1027
00:39:23,027 --> 00:39:24,820
He was a real nice guy,

1028
00:39:24,821 --> 00:39:27,656
came in with belly pain.

1029
00:39:27,657 --> 00:39:31,285
I saw a gall stone
on the ultrasound,

1030
00:39:31,286 --> 00:39:33,453
normal EKG, troponin.

1031
00:39:33,454 --> 00:39:36,832
The pain resolved,
and he was in the hall

1032
00:39:36,833 --> 00:39:38,710
when he had a cardiac arrest.

1033
00:39:41,504 --> 00:39:42,881
I couldn't save him.

1034
00:39:44,424 --> 00:39:45,884
People die.

1035
00:39:47,343 --> 00:39:50,846
And we do the best we can...

1036
00:39:50,847 --> 00:39:53,266
but people die.

1037
00:39:54,434 --> 00:39:56,436
- You ever get used to it?

1038
00:39:57,937 --> 00:39:59,564
- [chuckles] No.

1039
00:40:00,940 --> 00:40:03,775
No, you...

1040
00:40:03,776 --> 00:40:05,695
you try to accept it.

1041
00:40:07,697 --> 00:40:09,449
You try to find balance.

1042
00:40:14,871 --> 00:40:17,790
- I don't know if I can take
another day like today.

1043
00:40:19,334 --> 00:40:22,711
Primary care specialty
sounds way better.

1044
00:40:22,712 --> 00:40:24,504
Pedes, maybe.

1045
00:40:24,505 --> 00:40:26,049
- Yeah, maybe.

1046
00:40:27,550 --> 00:40:30,720
I know I'd be bored
out of my fucking mind.

1047
00:40:32,013 --> 00:40:36,058
Seriously, I like the challenge
of undiagnosed illness,

1048
00:40:36,059 --> 00:40:41,063
of quick decisions,
of life-saving procedures.

1049
00:40:41,064 --> 00:40:43,482
And I like being here
for people

1050
00:40:43,483 --> 00:40:45,902
on the worst days
of their lives.

1051
00:40:50,740 --> 00:40:52,033
[clears throat]

1052
00:40:54,077 --> 00:40:56,078
You know what I think?

1053
00:40:56,079 --> 00:40:57,788
- What?

1054
00:40:57,789 --> 00:41:00,123
- I think you should go home.

1055
00:41:00,124 --> 00:41:01,583
I think you should sleep on it.

1056
00:41:01,584 --> 00:41:03,085
And I think you should
at least wait

1057
00:41:03,086 --> 00:41:04,294
till the morning to decide

1058
00:41:04,295 --> 00:41:05,755
if you want to come back.

1059
00:41:08,758 --> 00:41:11,093
- I don't really want to...

1060
00:41:11,094 --> 00:41:13,720
go back in there
and see anybody.

1061
00:41:13,721 --> 00:41:14,931
- Yeah.

1062
00:41:16,432 --> 00:41:18,976
OK, pull off your gown.
I'll take it in.

1063
00:41:18,977 --> 00:41:21,436
Shift is over.

1064
00:41:21,437 --> 00:41:22,939
Go home.

1065
00:41:24,899 --> 00:41:27,944
[indistinct chatter]

1066
00:41:35,785 --> 00:41:37,119
- Oh!
- [gasps] Oh, God.

1067
00:41:37,120 --> 00:41:38,161
- No, I'm--I'm--
- Sorry.

1068
00:41:38,162 --> 00:41:39,621
- I'm good. I'm good. I'm good.

1069
00:41:39,622 --> 00:41:41,289
- There's a dummy
in a wheelchair.

1070
00:41:41,290 --> 00:41:43,209
- Uh, yeah, he moves around.

1071
00:41:46,295 --> 00:41:48,463
I see I've been replaced.

1072
00:41:48,464 --> 00:41:50,007
- Oh, did I take your spot?

1073
00:41:50,008 --> 00:41:51,675
- Not really, no.
- Are you sure?

1074
00:41:51,676 --> 00:41:53,135
- Where the hell have you been?

1075
00:41:53,136 --> 00:41:54,803
- I found Ogilvie.

1076
00:41:54,804 --> 00:41:56,054
- And?

1077
00:41:56,055 --> 00:41:58,390
- And he's going home.

1078
00:41:58,391 --> 00:41:59,641
- That's it?

1079
00:41:59,642 --> 00:42:00,976
- It's a long story.

1080
00:42:00,977 --> 00:42:02,394
- Well, I got nothing but time.

1081
00:42:02,395 --> 00:42:04,563
- I need to respect
his privacy.

1082
00:42:04,564 --> 00:42:06,148
- Where's the fun in that?

1083
00:42:06,149 --> 00:42:08,151
- Beep. Beep, beep. [chuckles]
- Sorry.

1084
00:42:09,402 --> 00:42:11,446
[whirring]

1085
00:42:16,701 --> 00:42:18,160
- Um, the shredder jammed.

1086
00:42:18,161 --> 00:42:21,163
- Yeah, uh, try
putting it in reverse.

1087
00:42:21,164 --> 00:42:23,498
[whirring]

1088
00:42:23,499 --> 00:42:24,708
- Perfect.

1089
00:42:24,709 --> 00:42:26,835
- Just give it
a little love tap.

1090
00:42:26,836 --> 00:42:28,378
- OK.
- Kick it.

1091
00:42:28,379 --> 00:42:29,504
[banging]

1092
00:42:29,505 --> 00:42:31,131
Jesus Christ.

1093
00:42:31,132 --> 00:42:33,008
[laughter]

1094
00:42:33,009 --> 00:42:35,177
- Guess Mr. Digby got
a room upstairs.

1095
00:42:35,178 --> 00:42:36,678
- In your dreams.

1096
00:42:36,679 --> 00:42:38,680
- He's not in South 21.

1097
00:42:38,681 --> 00:42:40,057
- Yeah, he is.

1098
00:42:40,058 --> 00:42:42,392
- There's a new guy in the bed.

1099
00:42:42,393 --> 00:42:44,394
- Short hair, clean-shaved?

1100
00:42:44,395 --> 00:42:46,855
I'll take care of it.

1101
00:42:46,856 --> 00:42:48,523
- Scout film showed
obvious pathology,

1102
00:42:48,524 --> 00:42:50,067
so we ordered the CT angiogram.

1103
00:42:50,068 --> 00:42:51,735
- Textbook ascending
aortic aneurysm.

1104
00:42:51,736 --> 00:42:53,570
Eight centimeters.

1105
00:42:53,571 --> 00:42:55,447
50% one-year mortality.

1106
00:42:55,448 --> 00:42:56,740
- So he definitely needs
surgery.

1107
00:42:56,741 --> 00:42:57,741
- If he wants to live.

1108
00:42:57,742 --> 00:42:58,784
- OK, well, he's a friend,

1109
00:42:58,785 --> 00:43:00,077
so I will talk to him.

1110
00:43:00,078 --> 00:43:01,244
I'll get him admitted
to your service.

1111
00:43:01,245 --> 00:43:03,371
- Not today.
- Why not?

1112
00:43:03,372 --> 00:43:05,207
- He needs to see Cardiology
and Pulmonary first

1113
00:43:05,208 --> 00:43:06,458
to be cleared for the OR.

1114
00:43:06,459 --> 00:43:08,376
They can do a stress echo,
PFTs.

1115
00:43:08,377 --> 00:43:10,629
- He needs to be admitted
and evaluated as an inpatient.

1116
00:43:10,630 --> 00:43:12,297
- Nothing ever gets done
over a holiday weekend.

1117
00:43:12,298 --> 00:43:13,381
He can take it easy at home.

1118
00:43:13,382 --> 00:43:14,800
- He's a ticking time bomb.

1119
00:43:14,801 --> 00:43:16,134
- He's probably been that way
for years.

1120
00:43:16,135 --> 00:43:17,427
We can get him in
early next week

1121
00:43:17,428 --> 00:43:19,054
with a specialist
to get his testing done.

1122
00:43:19,055 --> 00:43:21,014
All goes well, he's on
the OR schedule in a week.

1123
00:43:21,015 --> 00:43:22,599
- What happens if
it ruptures before then?

1124
00:43:22,600 --> 00:43:24,434
- Call 911.

1125
00:43:24,435 --> 00:43:26,103
Sorry, Robby. Best we can do.

1126
00:43:26,104 --> 00:43:28,064
Text me his number.
I'll have my office coordinate.

1127
00:43:30,233 --> 00:43:31,900
- Fuck!
- Hey!

1128
00:43:31,901 --> 00:43:33,611
Take a walk. Come on!

1129
00:43:37,073 --> 00:43:38,740
[bell dings]
- Two for discharge.

1130
00:43:38,741 --> 00:43:40,408
- Yeah, please don't
ring the bell.

1131
00:43:40,409 --> 00:43:42,035
The computers are
back up and running.

1132
00:43:42,036 --> 00:43:43,578
- Not for these two.

1133
00:43:43,579 --> 00:43:44,955
They don't have electronic
health records yet.

1134
00:43:44,956 --> 00:43:46,373
- Wait, hold up.

1135
00:43:46,374 --> 00:43:48,583
This kid had
a wrist X-ray ordered.

1136
00:43:48,584 --> 00:43:49,876
Ugh, it never happened.

1137
00:43:49,877 --> 00:43:51,169
And water-skiing lady's

1138
00:43:51,170 --> 00:43:52,504
still waiting on a knee series.

1139
00:43:52,505 --> 00:43:53,755
- I canceled the X-rays.

1140
00:43:53,756 --> 00:43:56,091
Diagnosed them both
with ultrasound.

1141
00:43:56,092 --> 00:43:57,342
- Really?

1142
00:43:57,343 --> 00:43:59,094
- Yeah.

1143
00:43:59,095 --> 00:44:01,596
The, uh, little boy had
a simple buckle fracture.

1144
00:44:01,597 --> 00:44:04,182
Distal radius.
He got a Velcro splint.

1145
00:44:04,183 --> 00:44:06,935
And Mrs. Stegman had
a small medial meniscus tear.

1146
00:44:06,936 --> 00:44:09,312
Knee immobilizer, crutches,
and a follow up with ortho.

1147
00:44:09,313 --> 00:44:11,648
- Could have used you
on the day shift today.

1148
00:44:11,649 --> 00:44:14,109
- They don't teach us a lot
of musculoskeletal ultrasound.

1149
00:44:14,110 --> 00:44:15,610
- I know.

1150
00:44:15,611 --> 00:44:18,280
That's why I did electives
at Harvard and Highland.

1151
00:44:18,281 --> 00:44:21,116
- Are you applying for
an ultrasound fellowship here?

1152
00:44:21,117 --> 00:44:23,910
- I am,
but it is so competitive.

1153
00:44:23,911 --> 00:44:26,121
- That's what I hear.
- Kind of a long shot.

1154
00:44:26,122 --> 00:44:27,581
I only have one publication,

1155
00:44:27,582 --> 00:44:29,457
but it's a series
of case reports diagnosing

1156
00:44:29,458 --> 00:44:32,002
shoulder dislocations
with ultrasound only.

1157
00:44:32,003 --> 00:44:34,004
If you want to learn some MSK,
you can tag along.

1158
00:44:34,005 --> 00:44:35,505
I got three more to scan.

1159
00:44:35,506 --> 00:44:37,507
- Maybe some other time.

1160
00:44:37,508 --> 00:44:39,968
- Cool.

1161
00:44:39,969 --> 00:44:42,304
[whirring]

1162
00:44:42,305 --> 00:44:44,306
- Hey, don't jam the shredder.

1163
00:44:44,307 --> 00:44:45,807
What is that, two charts?

1164
00:44:45,808 --> 00:44:48,352
- No, it's my ultrasound
fellowship application.

1165
00:44:50,146 --> 00:44:51,813
- Hey.

1166
00:44:51,814 --> 00:44:54,816
Uh, sorry about your patient.

1167
00:44:54,817 --> 00:44:57,819
- Yeah, Orlando's
in pretty bad shape.

1168
00:44:57,820 --> 00:44:59,696
- Oh, no,
I--I was talking about

1169
00:44:59,697 --> 00:45:03,366
the triple-A guy
you worked on with Ogilvie.

1170
00:45:03,367 --> 00:45:04,493
[sighs]

1171
00:45:05,912 --> 00:45:08,538
He didn't make it
through surgery.

1172
00:45:08,539 --> 00:45:10,081
I tried to talk to Robby
about it,

1173
00:45:10,082 --> 00:45:12,083
but I think he's busy.

1174
00:45:12,084 --> 00:45:13,753
- Excuse me.

1175
00:45:16,047 --> 00:45:17,924
- [sighing] Oh, shit.

1176
00:45:22,094 --> 00:45:23,637
- You think I'm on edge?

1177
00:45:23,638 --> 00:45:25,388
First you're shaming Samira,
then McKay--

1178
00:45:25,389 --> 00:45:26,723
- They both needed
to be called out

1179
00:45:26,724 --> 00:45:28,058
for unacceptable behavior.
- Yeah?

1180
00:45:28,059 --> 00:45:29,351
Yeah, well, you do that
in private.

1181
00:45:29,352 --> 00:45:30,810
Same place
you share your thoughts

1182
00:45:30,811 --> 00:45:32,020
about a patient's
possible suicide.

1183
00:45:32,021 --> 00:45:33,480
And slamming stuff? Please.

1184
00:45:33,481 --> 00:45:34,689
Sign out all the shit
that's bugging you

1185
00:45:34,690 --> 00:45:35,857
and get out of here.

1186
00:45:35,858 --> 00:45:37,651
- I can't.
- Yes, you can.

1187
00:45:37,652 --> 00:45:39,027
When either of my kids
was acting like this,

1188
00:45:39,028 --> 00:45:40,487
I gave them a time-out
in their room.

1189
00:45:40,488 --> 00:45:41,655
- Whoa, whoa, whoa,
you're not my mother.

1190
00:45:41,656 --> 00:45:42,864
- Yeah? Well, too bad.

1191
00:45:42,865 --> 00:45:44,032
You need one.
- No, I had one.

1192
00:45:44,033 --> 00:45:45,742
She left.
I don't need another one.

1193
00:45:45,743 --> 00:45:47,077
What I need is someone
who can actually run this place

1194
00:45:47,078 --> 00:45:49,037
while I'm gone.
- OK, I'm sorry.

1195
00:45:49,038 --> 00:45:50,372
I didn't know.
- Nobody knows.

1196
00:45:50,373 --> 00:45:52,666
Who needs to know?
Who gives a fuck?

1197
00:45:52,667 --> 00:45:54,000
- I think you need a break.

1198
00:45:54,001 --> 00:45:55,543
- That is what
the sabbatical is for.

1199
00:45:55,544 --> 00:45:57,087
- Then start it now. Walk away.

1200
00:45:57,088 --> 00:45:58,380
- I have too much to do.

1201
00:45:58,381 --> 00:46:00,048
- Let someone else
talk to Duke.

1202
00:46:00,049 --> 00:46:01,800
- No, that needs
to come from me.

1203
00:46:01,801 --> 00:46:03,134
- Why?

1204
00:46:03,135 --> 00:46:04,719
- Because I owe him that.

1205
00:46:04,720 --> 00:46:06,554
It needs to come from a friend,
not a stranger,

1206
00:46:06,555 --> 00:46:08,598
otherwise he's gonna bail
and drop dead while I'm gone.

1207
00:46:08,599 --> 00:46:11,476
- OK, wrap that up
and then leave.

1208
00:46:11,477 --> 00:46:13,103
- It's not just Duke.

1209
00:46:13,104 --> 00:46:14,854
I'm not sure that Al-Hashimi
is fit to run this place.

1210
00:46:14,855 --> 00:46:16,064
- What's that supposed to mean?
- I'm not sure.

1211
00:46:16,065 --> 00:46:17,691
I'm trying to figure that out.

1212
00:46:17,692 --> 00:46:18,942
Did you know that she wants
to have two attendings

1213
00:46:18,943 --> 00:46:20,568
on at all times?
- No.

1214
00:46:20,569 --> 00:46:22,070
- Is that something
she's worked out with Gloria?

1215
00:46:22,071 --> 00:46:23,613
- I don't know.
- I also don't know

1216
00:46:23,614 --> 00:46:24,948
if Langdon is going to relapse.

1217
00:46:24,949 --> 00:46:25,949
I don't know if
Whitaker's gonna be able

1218
00:46:25,950 --> 00:46:27,367
to take care of my shit.

1219
00:46:27,368 --> 00:46:28,910
I don't know if
Javadi's gonna give up on

1220
00:46:28,911 --> 00:46:30,787
what she's good at
or if Samira's gonna flame out

1221
00:46:30,788 --> 00:46:32,247
because of some bullshit
with her mother.

1222
00:46:32,248 --> 00:46:34,624
- Is there anything else?
- Yeah, you.

1223
00:46:34,625 --> 00:46:36,876
I don't know about you
running around

1224
00:46:36,877 --> 00:46:38,628
with a full syringe of Versed
in your pocket.

1225
00:46:38,629 --> 00:46:41,047
I'm worried about the people
that I care about.

1226
00:46:41,048 --> 00:46:43,425
- We'll all manage
until you come back.

1227
00:46:43,426 --> 00:46:44,592
We always do.

1228
00:46:44,593 --> 00:46:46,429
- Yeah?
What if I don't come back?

1229
00:46:57,064 --> 00:47:00,025
<i>[pensive music]</i>

1230
00:47:00,026 --> 00:47:07,074
<i>♪ ♪</i>

